WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:22.330
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life from WBEZ Chicago distributed by Public Radio international. And today's show begins with a question that somehow independently popped up in the heads of five different people all around the same time. The people were Caitlin Kenney, Dave Kestenbaum, Alex Blumberg, Jacob Goldstein, and Adam Davidson. All of them cover economics for our show and for NPR news as part of the Planet Money project.
<v Ira Glass>这里是来自WBEZ芝加哥的《美国生活》，节目由国际公共广播电台发行。今天的节目从一个碰巧在同一时期独立出现在五个不同头脑中的问题开始。这五个人是凯特琳·肯尼、戴夫·克斯滕鲍姆、亚历克斯·布隆伯格、雅各布·戈尔德斯坦和亚当·戴维森。他们所有人都在我们的节目以及NPR新闻的“金钱星球”项目中负责经济报道。

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:24.100
<v Caitlin Kenney>It was in November of 2008.
<v Caitlin Kenney>那是2008年11月。

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.520
<v Male Speaker 1>Yeah, it was in December of 2008.
<v Male Speaker 1>对，那是2008年12月。

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.690
<v Male Speaker 2>It was in the heart of the financial crisis.
<v Male Speaker 2>那是金融危机的核心时期。

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:31.360
<v Jacob Goldstein>Lehman brothers went bust.
<v Jacob Goldstein>雷曼兄弟倒闭了。

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:41.900
<v Dave Kestenbaum>You were hearing on the news every day, all these like crazy numbers. X trillion dollars lost when the housing bubble popped. A trillion dollars disappeared from the stock market this week.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>你每天在新闻里听到的都是这些疯狂的数字。当房地产泡沫破裂时，数万亿美元蒸发了。这周股票市场上消失了一万亿美元。

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:49.590
<v Male Speaker 2>My family members, my wife, my friends, people were just asking me, where did that money go? Who got it?
<v Male Speaker 2>我的家人、我的妻子、我的朋友，人们都在问我，那些钱去哪儿了？谁得到了？

00:00:49.590 --> 00:01:08.100
<v Dave Kestenbaum>You know, was there a black hole it went into? Was their a big fire somewhere, which burned up a lot of dollar bills. Where did the money go? I spent a long time thinking about that.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>你知道，是不是掉进黑洞里了？或者哪里有场大火，把一大堆美钞给烧了。钱到底去哪儿了？我花了很长时间思考这个问题。

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:30.580
<v Ira Glass>All these reporters but Adam were pretty new to reporting on money and finance at the time. And that last reporter you heard, Kestenbaum, went online and bought some play money so we could run little scenarios, pretending that person A took out a loan from person B to buy a house from person C to try to understand where all the money went when all the housing prices collapsed. Jacob meanwhile, went to dinner with an aunt of his.
<v Ira Glass>除了亚当之外，所有这些记者在当时对金钱和金融报道都相当陌生。你听到的最后一位记者克斯滕鲍姆，当时上网买了一些游戏币，这样我们就可以进行一些小场景模拟，假设A向B贷款买下C的一套房子，试图弄清楚当所有房价崩溃时，所有的钱都去哪儿了。与此同时，雅各布和他的姑姑一起吃了晚饭。

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:54.690
<v Jacob Goldstein>My aunt is an MBA and was a very successful businesswoman and made a lot of money. And I actually asked her because this idea that trillions of dollars could disappear from the stock market was confusing to me. And so I said where did all that money go? And she said, money is fiction.
<v Jacob Goldstein>我的姑姑有MBA学位，是一位非常成功的女商人，赚了很多钱。我真的问过她，因为股票市场能蒸发数万亿美元这个概念让我感到困惑。所以我问，所有这些钱都去哪儿了？她说，金钱是虚构的。

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.430
<v Ira Glass>What does that mean?
<v Ira Glass>这是什么意思？

00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:12.300
<v Jacob Goldstein>Well, I guess it was the fact-- like it would have been one thing if a yoga teacher or something said that to me. But this is like my businesswoman aunt.
<v Jacob Goldstein>嗯，我想是因为——如果是一个瑜伽老师或者什么人对我说这话是一回事。但这可是我的女商人姑姑啊。

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:58.550
<v Ira Glass>Money is fiction. So the answer to the question, where did the money go when the housing market collapsed turns out to be that the money never existed in the first place. All those houses used to be worth a certain amount and now they were worth a lower amount. Simply because that's what everyone now agreed. No money changed hands, no many vanished. Same with retirement funds and stock portfolios. Alex says that this is when he realized that no where was safe. That there were inflation even sitting in the bank. All his money value could just slowly vaporize. Money is not solid. Its value could disappear. And all these reporters says that after that they would be working on what seemed like perfectly ordinary stories asking straightforward questions and they would bump up against this weird, fictional quality that money has.
<v Ira Glass>金钱是虚构的。所以当房地产市场崩溃时，钱去哪儿了这个问题，答案是这些钱从一开始就不存在。所有那些房子过去只值一定金额，现在它们的价值变低了。仅仅因为那是现在所有人的共识。没有金钱易手，也没有金钱消失。退休基金和股票投资组合也是如此。亚历克斯说，正是在这个时候他意识到没有哪个地方是安全的。甚至放在银行里也会有通货膨胀。他所有的金钱价值都可能慢慢蒸发。金钱不是实体。它的价值可能会消失。所有这些记者都说，在那之后，他们会做一些看似非常普通、问着直截了当的问题的报道，然后他们就会撞上金钱这种怪异的、虚构的属性。

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:02.370
<v Ira Glass>For example, Kestenbaum says he was doing a story and he came upon this.
<v Ira Glass>例如，克斯滕鲍姆说他在做一个报道时遇到了这样一个情况。

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:15.090
<v Dave Kestenbaum>One question is how much money is out there in dollars? You would think we track that among all things, right? And yet, it's a really complicated question to answer.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>有一个问题是，到底有多少美元在流通？你可能会认为我们会在所有事情中追踪这个，对吧？然而，这是一个非常难回答的复杂问题。

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.970
<v Ira Glass>Why is that so complicated?
<v Ira Glass>为什么这这么复杂？

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:46.450
<v Dave Kestenbaum>You could imagine trying to answer it by asking everybody like how much money do they have. So you open your wallet, there's cash in there. And then you'd also probably add your checking account and your savings account. But the money in your checking account and your savings account, the bank is a bank. What it does is it takes that money in-- your money-- and then it loans it out t some guy who wants to open a shoe shining store down the street. So he's holding your cash.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>你可以想象通过问每个人他们有多少钱来试图回答这个问题。所以你打开钱包，里面有现金。然后你可能还会加上你的支票账户和储蓄账户。但是你支票账户和储蓄账户里的钱，银行就是银行。它的做法是把这笔钱——你的钱——拿进来，然后贷给街头某个想开擦鞋店的家伙。所以他拿着你的现金。

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:54.270
<v Ira Glass>Oh right. So the question is, how do you count that money without double counting it? Are you counting me having it or are you counting him having it?
<v Ira Glass>哦，对了。所以问题是，如何在不重复计算的情况下统计这些钱？你是算我有这笔钱，还是算他有这笔钱？

00:03:54.270 --> 00:04:26.110
<v Dave Kestenbaum>How do you handle that? And it gets really complicated because think of that guy who's opening that store who borrowed the money. He's going to pay someone to make a sign for him. That person's going to deposit some of that money in the bank. The bank's going to then loan that out to somebody else. In fact, if you look in the textbooks, there's a little footnote saying, sorry, we had to do a little math here. And they tell you how to sum an infinite series.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>你如何处理这种情况？这变得非常复杂，因为想想那个借了钱开店的家伙。他要付钱请人给他做个招牌。那个人会把一部分钱存入银行。然后银行会把这笔钱贷给其他人。事实上，如果你看教科书，会有一个小脚注说，抱歉，我们这里必须做点数学题。然后它们会教你如何计算一个无穷级数。

00:04:26.110 --> 00:05:04.170
<v Ira Glass>Jacob says that he just figured our money works this way because we're a complicated, industrial society with banks and stock markets and financial instruments of all kinds. And then he read something that made him realize that no, no, no. The fictional quality of money is inherent in the very idea of money. In any system of currency, no matter how simple, this aha moment came to him when he was reading this book from 1910 about an island called Yap in the South Pacific. At the time on this island, for currency, this pre-industrial people used something completely impractical and counter-intuitive, these massive stone sculptures in the shape of coins. And I mean massive.
<v Ira Glass>雅各布说，他之前只是觉得钱就是这样运作的，因为我们是一个拥有银行、股市和各种金融工具的复杂的工业社会。然后他读到了一些东西，让他意识到不，不，不。金钱的虚构属性是金钱这个概念本身固有的。在任何货币系统中，无论多么简单，当他读到这本1910年关于南太平洋一个叫雅浦岛的小岛的书时，这个顿悟时刻降临了。当时在这个岛上，作为货币，这些前工业时代的人使用了一种完全不切实际且反直觉的东西，这些巨大的硬币形状的石雕。我是说巨大的。

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:30.530
<v Jacob Goldstein>They're huge. The biggest ones are taller than a man. You know, they weigh more than a car. And they use them for big things, right? They wouldn't use them for little everyday purchases. You'd use it for a dowry or like this anthropologist told me if somebody from your village got killed in another village and you wanted to get his body back, like a warrior, you might use one of these stones to pay the other village to get your warrior's body back.
<v Jacob Goldstein>它们巨大无比。最大的比一个男人还高。你知道，它们比一辆汽车还重。它们被用来做大事，对吧？它们不会被用于日常的小额购买。你会用它来作聘礼，或者就像这位人类学家对我说的那样，如果你村里的人在另一个村子被杀，而你想把他的尸体要回来，比如一个勇士，你可能会用其中一块石头付给另一个村子，以换回你勇士的尸体。

00:05:30.530 --> 00:06:10.510
<v Jacob Goldstein>So there's this obvious problem. There's no machinery. These things weigh thousands of pounds. They have essentially an innovation. It's really a financial innovation, which is you don't have to actually have the stone to own the stone. The stone, like you know, it's sitting on a path or something and everybody just knows that I own it one day. And then you, you Ira, you kill the warrior from my village and I'm like OK. I want to pay you with this stone and you give me back my warrior. I get the warrior back, the stone doesn't have to move. It just sits there on that path, but now everybody knows that's Ira's stone. I don't own it any more.
<v Jacob Goldstein>所以这里有一个明显的问题。没有机械。这些东西重达数千磅。它们本质上有一项创新。这确实是一项金融创新，那就是你不需要真正拥有这块石头就能拥有这块石头。这块石头，你知道的，它就放在一条小路上什么的，每个人都知道那一天是我拥有的。然后你，你伊拉，你杀了我村里的勇士，我说好吧。我想用这块石头付给你，你把我的勇士还给我。我把勇士要回来了，石头不需要移动。它就静静地躺在那条路上，但现在大家都知道那是伊拉的石头了。我不再拥有它了。

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:23.010
<v Ira Glass>But Jacob says it gets even more interesting. To understand this next point you have to know first that the limestone that they use to make these giant sculpture coins, it wasn't actually found on Yap. They had to go to another island 250 miles away to make them.
<v Ira Glass>但雅各布说，事情变得更有趣了。要理解接下来这一点，你必须首先知道，他们用来制造这些巨型雕塑硬币的石灰石，实际上并不是在雅浦岛上发现的。他们必须去250英里外的另一个岛屿去制作它们。

00:06:23.010 --> 00:07:09.060
<v Jacob Goldstein>And these guys are going out in these little bamboo boats to some other island where they're carving these giant limestone disks. At one point, a crew had gone and cut one of these giant stones on this island and they were bringing it back across the sea. And just before they got back to Yap, there was some kind of storm, and so they had to cut the store loss or the stone went overboard or something. In any case, the stone ended up on the bottom of the sea. The crew themselves survived. They made it back to the island and they told everybody what had happened. And the people of Yap said, that's fine. That's no problem. We believe you and this stone money, this giant stone on the bottom of the sea, it exists. It's still good money. Somebody owns that stone, even though it's sitting on the bottom of the ocean.
<v Jacob Goldstein>这些家伙乘着小竹船去别的岛屿，在那里雕刻这些巨大的石灰石盘。有一次，一队人去那个岛上切割了一块这样的巨石，他们正把它运回海里。就在他们回到雅浦岛之前，遇到了一场风暴，所以他们不得不把石头割舍抛弃，或者石头落水了什么的。无论如何，这块石头最后落到了海底。船员们自己生还了。他们回到了岛上，把发生的事情告诉了大家。雅浦岛的人民说，没关系。那不是问题。我们相信你，这块石头钱，这块海底的巨石，它存在着。它依然是好钱。有人拥有那块石头，尽管它正静静地躺在海洋深处。

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:14.010
<v Ira Glass>What?
<v Ira Glass>什么？

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:24.100
<v Jacob Goldstein>But it makes sense, right? One reaction is like, oh my God, these people are crazy. But it's like a logical extension of what they're already doing.
<v Jacob Goldstein>但这说得通，对吧？一种反应会是，天哪，这些人疯了。但这就像是他们已经在做的事情的逻辑延伸。

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:55.240
<v Ira Glass>In fact, Jacob says it's not logically different from what we do. For money after all, long ago, we used to use gold. And if you wanted to buy something you'd have to carry around these heavy, shiny pieces of metal. And then we decided, no, let's just leave the gold in a bank. Instead of the gold what we're going to carry around are these pieces of paper. And the paper on them says, yes, there's gold. You can take this paper money to a bank. You can swap it for gold. Maybe you've seen old dollars that say on them promise to pay the bearer so many dollars in gold. You could swap it.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，雅各布说，这在逻辑上与我们的做法没有区别。因为毕竟很久以前，我们用的是黄金。如果你想买东西，你得随身携带这些沉重、闪亮的金属块。然后我们决定，不，我们还是把黄金留在家里吧。我们随身携带的将不再是黄金，而是这些纸片。纸片上写着，是的，有黄金。你可以把这纸币拿到银行去。你可以把它换成黄金。也许你见过老旧的美元，上面写着承诺向持票人支付相应数量的黄金。你可以兑换它。

00:07:55.240 --> 00:08:09.880
<v Ira Glass>Then we decided, it was the year 1933. We decided you can't trade in dollar bills from gold anymore in this country. Dollar bills are just going to represent the idea of money. That's it. Not gold. They're just money.
<v Ira Glass>然后我们决定，那是1933年。我们决定在这个国家你不能再用纸币兑换黄金了。纸币将只代表金钱的概念。就是这样。不是黄金。它们只是金钱。

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:13.330
<v Ira Glass>And when I talked about this with Jacob he said, it gets even more abstract.
<v Ira Glass>当我和雅各布谈论这个时，他说，这变得更加抽象了。

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:41.760
<v Jacob Goldstein>Because now, if you think of most of the money that you have, or most of the money that I have, it's never currency. I get paid. That is just a direct deposit from NPR, from my employer, to my checking account. It's not like they give me a bunch of hundred dollar bills every week. And then, I pay my bills online. So currency even now is like old-fashioned. You don't have to touch money. You don't have to see it. It's just information.
<v Jacob Goldstein>因为现在，如果你想想你拥有的绝大多数钱，或者我拥有的绝大多数钱，它从来都不是实体货币。我发工资了。那只是来自NPR、来自我雇主的直接存款，存入我的支票账户。并不是说他们每周给我一堆一百美元的钞票。然后，我在网上支付账单。所以现在的实体货币甚至都显得过时了。你不需要触摸金钱。你不需要看到它。它只是信息。

00:08:41.760 --> 00:09:00.220
<v Ira Glass>Until this conversation it never occurred to me that money doesn't change hands when we pay our bills online or anything. Like I don't know why I never even stopped to think, right, at no point does the bank go and deliver like $100 to the phone company on my behalf. It really is just numbers going back and forth in the computer.
<v Ira Glass>直到这次对话，我才突然意识到，当我们在线支付账单或做其他事情时，金钱根本没有易手。就像我不知道为什么我甚至从未停下来想过，对啊，银行在任何时候都没有代表我去把100美元送到电话公司。这真的只是电脑里来回跳动的数字。

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:22.640
<v Jacob Goldstein>Yeah. Nothing happens in the physical world. I mean I guess, except electrical pulses I guess it would be flying back and forth between computers. The money doesn't really exist. Not only is there no gold. There aren't even bills for most of the money that exists. Most of the money that exists is just the idea. It's just the bank saying, yes, there is this much money in your account.
<v Jacob Goldstein>对。物理世界中什么也没发生。我的意思是，我想除了在电脑之间飞来飞去的电脉冲之外。这笔钱并不是真的存在。不仅没有黄金。对于存在的绝大多数金钱来说，甚至连纸币都没有。绝大多数存在的金钱都只是一个概念。它只是银行在说，是的，你的账户里有这么多钱。

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:50.140
<v Ira Glass>Well today on our program, the most stoner question we have ever posed on our show, what is money? That's right. What is money? That's the question we're asking. I know how it sounds. The fact that the money that we use every day is about as real as those stones at the bottom of the sea, that makes our lives easier in all kinds of ways, but it is also incredibly dangerous that that is the nature of money as you'll hear today in two action-filled acts.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，今天在我们的节目中，我们要提出节目有史以来最像瘾君子的终极问题：什么是金钱？没错了。什么是金钱？这就是我们正在提出的问题。我知道这听起来怎么样。我们每天使用的金钱和海底那些石头一样真实，这一点在各种方面让我们的生活变得更容易，但这也是极其危险的，因为这就是金钱的本质，正如你今天将在两个充满行动的幕中所听到的一样。

00:09:50.140 --> 00:10:04.420
<v Ira Glass>When you understand the money is created out of nothing, you understand all kinds of things. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>当你明白金钱是凭空创造出来的，你就理解了形形色色的事物。请继续收听。

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:08.410
<v Ira Glass>Act 1: The lie that saved Brazil.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：拯救巴西的谎言。

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:21.660
<v Ira Glass>Well let's begin with a trip to a country where the fiction that is money. That fiction, that set of shared beliefs that is a fiction completely fell apart. Everyone stopped believing in their currency and of course, that had disastrous results.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，让我们从前往一个国家开始，那里的货币虚构性。那种虚构，那种作为虚构的共同信念完全崩溃了。每个人都不再相信他们的货币，当然，那带来了灾难性的后果。

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:42.310
<v Ira Glass>The same country in a truly incredible piece of policy making, you almost never hear of any government anywhere solving a problem so thoroughly and so well. The government tricked a hundred fifty million people into believing again, that their money was worth something when there was absolutely no evidence to support that claim. And it worked. Here's Chana Joffe-Walt.
<v Ira Glass>同一个国家，通过一项真正不可思议的政策制定，你几乎从未听说过任何地方的任何政府能如此彻底、如此出色地解决一个问题。政府欺骗了一亿五千万人，让他们重新相信他们的货币是有价值的，而当时绝对没有任何证据支持这种说法。而且它成功了。这是查娜·乔夫-沃尔特。

00:10:42.310 --> 00:11:16.020
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>When you know the story I'm about to tell you, it really makes every single mundane commercial experience in Brazil seem like a complete miracle. Car commercials, miracle. The mall is a miracle. The guy in a bright red t-shirt in the mall who shoves paperwork into my hands and offers me a credit card, miracle. The credit card is a brand new concept in Brazil. And actually, everything that happens in this mall is new. The way everyone spends money in the mall is new. You can walk away with new shoes or a new backpack today and pay for it later.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>当你了解了我即将告诉你的这个故事，它真的让巴西每一个平凡的商业体验都看起来像是一个彻底的奇迹。汽车广告，奇迹。商场是奇迹。商场里那个穿着亮红色T恤、把一叠单子塞进我手里并向我推销信用卡的家伙，奇迹。信用卡在巴西是一个全新的概念。事实上，这个商场里发生的一切都是崭新的。每个人在商场里花钱的方式都是崭新的。你今天可以拿着新鞋或新背包离开，以后再付款。

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:18.360
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[SPEAKING PORTUGUESE]
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>[说葡萄牙语]

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:21.030
<v Translator>So a soccer ball in two installments.
<v Translator>所以一个足球分两期付款。

00:11:21.030 --> 00:11:24.460
<v Translator>[SPEAKING PORTUGUESE]
<v Translator>[说葡萄牙语]

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:28.470
<v Translator>Any shoes, the average is six installments.
<v Translator>任何鞋子，平均是六期。

00:11:28.470 --> 00:11:30.660
<v Translator>[SPEAKING PORTUGUESE]
<v Translator>[说葡萄牙语]

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:33.380
<v Translator>So a backpack, six installments.
<v Translator>所以一个背包，分六期。

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:35.710
<v Translator>[SPEAKING PORTUGUESE]
<v Translator>[说葡萄牙语]

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:37.650
<v Translator>All products.
<v Translator>所有产品。

00:11:37.650 --> 00:11:38.600
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Everything?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>全部？

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:40.020
<v Translator>Yeah.
<v Translator>对。

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:51.860
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>As I said that-- everything-- I was holding up a pair of sunglasses off of his display. "Yes," he told me, "six payments. Take them home today. Pay in six monthly installments later."
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>正如我所说的那——全部——我正从他的展示架上拿起一副太阳镜。“是的，”他告诉我，“分六次付款。今天带回家。一个月后开始分六个月付款。”

00:11:51.860 --> 00:12:06.280
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>In the past, you never would have had this, because Brazil had incredibly high inflation. In 1990, inflation in Brazil was 80% a month. Not a year, a month. So think about those sunglasses.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在过去，你绝对不会有这种待遇，因为巴西有着高得惊人的通货膨胀。1990年，巴西的通货膨胀率是每月80%。不是每年，是每月。所以想想那副太阳镜。

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:47.990
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Say they're selling for $10. One month later, with 80% inflation, the price is $18. Six months later the sunglasses are $340. And by the end of the year, that price tag reads more than $10,000. Brazilians lived with high inflation like this for decades. They could not figure out how to fix it. And they might still be living with it if it weren't for some unlikely heroes. Actually, the most unlikely group of national heroes you can imagine. Four former drinking buddies from grad school with a crazy plan, who were suddenly put in charge of the country's biggest economic crisis ever.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>假设它们的售价是10美元。一个月后，在80%的通货膨胀下，价格是18美元。六个月后，太阳镜是340美元。到了年底，那个价格标签上显示的数字将超过10,000美元。巴西人就这样生活在高通胀中几十年。他们一直想不出该如何解决这个问题。如果不是因为一些意想不到的英雄，他们可能至今仍生活在其中。事实上，这是你能想象到的最不可能成为国家英雄的群体。四个来自研究生的前酒友，带着一个疯狂的计划，突然被任命来处理该国历史上最大的经济危机。

00:12:47.990 --> 00:13:11.420
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But before we meet them and hear their story, let's talk about the way it used to be. So remember, prices were going up every day. Think about what that actually means in the supermarket. They had to change the prices every day. Ketano Ferare, this flirtatious 75-year-old in Sao Paulo remembers that that was someone's job. To walk the aisles and change the prices.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但在我们认识他们并听他们的故事之前，先来谈谈过去的生活方式。所以记住，价格每天都在上涨。想想这在超市里实际意味着什么。他们必须每天更改价格。凯塔诺·费拉雷，圣保罗这位风情万种的75岁老人记得那是某人的工作。走过货架并更改价格。

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:21.640
<v Ketano Ferare>There was a guy who changed the sticker. Plop, plop, plop, plop, plop. You passed the guy. And you buy things.
<v Ketano Ferare>有一个家伙负责换标签。啪，啪，啪，啪，啪。你从那个家伙身边走过。然后你买东西。

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:25.210
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Wait, you would walk by the guy? You would like get in front of him?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>等等，你会从那个家伙旁边走过？你会走到他前面去吗？

00:13:25.210 --> 00:13:26.190
<v Ketano Ferare>You run.
<v Ketano Ferare>你跑。

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:27.110
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>In front of him?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在他前面？

00:13:27.110 --> 00:13:31.110
<v Ketano Ferare>In front of the guy and buy things like that.
<v Ketano Ferare>在那个家伙前面，然后那样买东西。

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:34.800
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So that you could get to the goods before he changed the price?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这样你就能在他改价格之前买到商品？

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:36.750
<v Ketano Ferare>Yes, like that.
<v Ketano Ferare>对，就像那样。

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:41.860
<v Maria>And the whole day this machine is doing this.
<v Maria>整整一天这台机器都在这么干。

00:13:41.860 --> 00:14:05.500
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>This is Maria Leopoldina Biarenbeck. Someone with a very Brazilian long name, who spent a lot of time in supermarkets during this period. Maria was a housewife with four kids. She wasn't the running type. She would politely ask the sticker man to stop and wait. And if he didn't, she had another trick. She'd pull off his new sticker, walk up to the register, and pay the old price.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这是玛丽亚·莱奥波尔迪纳·比亚伦贝克。一个有着非常典型的长长巴西名字的人，在这期间花了很多时间在超市里。玛丽亚是一位育有四个孩子的家庭主妇。她不是那种会跑的人。她会礼貌地请贴标签的人停下来等等。如果他不听，她还有另一招。她会撕下他的新标签，走到收银台前，按照旧价格付款。

00:14:05.500 --> 00:14:19.040
<v Maria>But then they discovered the maquinilla the small machine. And that you couldn't do anything because it printed the price. That wasn't easy to take off.
<v Maria>但随后他们发现了报价机，那种小型机器。而且你什么也做不了，因为它打印出的价格根本不容易撕下来。

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:46.820
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Brazil's problem with inflation started all the way back in the 1950s. The president wanted to build a massive city in the middle of the jungle, a new capital, Brasilia. It would cost a lot of money and the government didn't have the money to do it, so it did what governments often do when they have a dream they can't afford. They essentially, printed the money, created it. Now the problem with doing this of course is inflation.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>巴西的通货膨胀问题要追溯到20世纪50年代。当时的总统想要在丛林中央建造一座庞大的城市，也就是新首都巴西利亚。这需要耗费巨资，而政府并没有这笔资金，于是政府做出了当他们负担不起某个梦想时经常做的事。他们基本上印制了货币，凭空创造了货币。而这么做带来的问题，当然就是通货膨胀。

00:14:46.820 --> 00:15:08.870
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>If there's say, a hundred dollars in the economy, you create a hundred more, now every dollar is worth half as much. That's inflation. And in Brazil, inflation continued for the next five decades. Year after year, Brazilian money was worth less and less. And this causes all sorts of problems, not just with the sticker man.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>比方说，如果经济中原本有一百美元，你又凭空创造了一百美元，现在每一美元的价值就缩水了一半。这就是通货膨胀。在巴西，通货膨胀在接下来的五十年里一直持续着。年复一年，巴西货币越来越不值钱。这引发了各种各样的问题，不仅仅是改价员的工作那么简单。

00:15:08.870 --> 00:15:29.920
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Say you get a $1,000 bonus at work. You put that money in a drawer. A year later it's worth a fraction of what it was. The minute you get paid the clock is ticking on your money. I talked to one man who used to have nightmares about money sitting on top of his dresser. Sitting still, just losing value.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>假设你在工作中获得了1000美元的奖金。你把这笔钱放进抽屉里。一年后，它的价值只剩下原本的一小部分。你拿到工资的那一刻起，你钱的价值就开始进入倒计时。我曾采访过一个人，他过去常常梦见钱放在梳妆台顶上。静静地躺在那里，不断贬值。

00:15:29.920 --> 00:15:42.530
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>A beer manufacturer told me he just stopped making beer because making beer just takes too long. You'd buy all the wheat and the hops and by the time it was brewed, everything was worth so much less.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>一个啤酒制造商告诉我，他干脆停止酿造啤酒了，因为酿造啤酒花的时间太长了。等你买齐了小麦和啤酒花，等啤酒酿好时，所有的东西都已经严重贬值了。

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:50.100
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>By the late '80s, inflation was the number one political issue. And so began the plan to fix it.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>到了80年代末，通货膨胀已经成了头号政治议题。解决它的计划也由此拉开序幕。

00:15:50.100 --> 00:16:10.790
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Now it turns out one of the best people to talk to about this is Maria Leopoldina Biarenbeck, the housewife who peeled off the stickers. Maria can take you through a detailed history of each president's failed plan to stop inflation. But you have to ask Maria every question twice because the first time she always answers like this.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>现在看来，关于这件事最合适的人选之一就是玛丽亚·莱奥波尔迪纳·比亚伦贝克，那个曾经撕下标签的主妇。玛丽亚可以带你回顾每一位总统失败的抗通胀计划的详细历史。但你必须把每个问题问玛丽亚两遍，因为她第一次总是这样回答。

00:16:10.790 --> 00:16:16.700
<v Maria>I don't know because I never had to do anything. I was just a plain housewife and mother.
<v Maria>我不知道，因为我从来不需要做什么。我只是一个普通的家庭主妇和母亲。

00:16:16.700 --> 00:16:42.700
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And then proceeds to be the most knowledgeable person you will speak to on the topic. OK, so first up she says, President Sarney in 1985. His solution? Simple. Businesses are raising prices, make that illegal. There was a price freeze. The problem with that was no one wanted to sell anything at the frozen price. All the merchants were just waiting for the day when prices would eventually go up again.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>然后她就会滔滔不绝，成为你在这个话题上能聊到的最博学的人。好的，首先她提到，1985年的萨尔内总统。他的解决方案？很简单。企业在涨价，那就把涨价定为非法。当时实施了价格冻结。这么做的问题在于，没有人愿意以被冻结的价格出售任何东西。所有的商家都在等待价格最终重新上涨的那一天。

00:16:42.700 --> 00:17:05.610
<v Maria>And you know what happened. People hid the merchandise. And you couldn't buy anything because they wanted the prices to grow up because the situation was a fantasy. It was not real. You couldn't find meat at the butcher's.
<v Maria>你知道结果怎么样。人们把商品藏了起来。你什么也买不到，因为他们希望价格上涨，整场局势就像是一场幻想。那不是真实的。你在肉铺里根本买不到肉。

00:17:05.610 --> 00:17:08.000
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>They just weren't buying meat to sell?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他们只是不买肉来卖吗？

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:09.480
<v Maria>They hid the cattle.
<v Maria>他们把牛藏起来了。

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:13.960
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So they hid the cattle waiting for the price freeze to go away?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以他们把牛藏起来，等着价格冻结解除？

00:17:13.960 --> 00:17:14.460
<v Maria>Yes.
<v Maria>是的。

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:14.930
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Really?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>真的吗？

00:17:14.930 --> 00:17:18.610
<v Maria>Yes. You can do that here, it's a very large country.
<v Maria>是的。你在这里完全可以做到，这是一个非常广袤的国家。

00:17:18.610 --> 00:17:31.070
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So that failed. And then came President Collor in 1990. He had a plan to solve inflation, but there was one catch. You're not going to be able to take your money out of the bank for a while.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以那个计划失败了。接着是1990年的科洛尔总统。他有一个解决通胀的计划，但有一个附加条件。有一段时间，你的钱将无法从银行取出来。

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:43.150
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Now Maria is telling me this and my translator, Flavio Ferreira, who is sitting in on our conversation could no longer keep quiet. He remembers that moment when the minister of finance made the announcement.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>现在玛丽亚正在告诉我这些，而坐在旁边旁听我们谈话的我的翻译弗拉维奥·费雷拉再也坐不住了。他记起了财政部长宣布那个消息的时刻。

00:17:43.150 --> 00:18:28.880
<v Flavio>And I remember the day when she was on TV explaining that they were going to confiscate everybody's money. So next day's banks would not work. I remember the face of that woman. She had studied in the best schools and she had been a professor at [INAUDIBLE]. And she was explaining to the nation as an economist why we need this. To end inflation we need the country to be together with us. But I remember I'm looking at her and said, God, a government cannot do that. I mean when a government does that, you lose people's respect.
<v Flavio>我记得她上电视解释他们将没收所有人存款的那天。所以第二天的银行无法正常营业。我记得那个女人的脸。她曾在最好的学校深造，还曾担任过某高校的教授。她作为一名经济学家，向全国解释我们为什么需要这样做。为了终结通胀，我们需要全国人民与我们同心协力。但我记得我看着她，心想，天哪，政府怎么能做出这种事。我的意思是，当政府这么做时，你就会失去人们的尊重。

00:18:28.880 --> 00:18:35.950
<v Maria>Oh, it was terrible. It was terrible. So many people committed suicide.
<v Maria>哦，太可怕了。太可怕了。有那么多人都自杀。

00:18:35.950 --> 00:19:12.940
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>When you mess with people's money, it doesn't go well. The economy went off a cliff. President Collor was impeached. And there was a new president and a new finance minister, and inflation kept rising. The Brazilian economy was at a low point. It looked like there was nothing to be done to fix it. Enter our heroes, those four economists we talked about at the beginning, who basically enter the picture now because the new finance minister knew nothing about economics. Which is why, on March 27 of 1993, he called one of our heroes, Edmar Bacha.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>当你打乱人们的钱财时，结果往往不会好。经济直接跌入谷底。科洛尔总统遭到弹劾。随后迎来了新总统和新财政部长，而通货膨胀仍在继续攀升。巴西经济陷入低谷。当时看来，似乎已经没有什么办法能拯救它了。我们的英雄们登场了，也就是我们在开头提到的那四位经济学家，他们之所以现在切入这个局面，是因为新上任的财政部长对经济学一窍不通。正因如此，在1993年3月27日，他打电话给我们的英雄之一，埃德马尔·巴沙。

00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:39.920
<v Edmar Basha>I was in my office at that university here, at the Catholic university. And I got the call soon after I had finished teaching a class. And he said, well, I've just been named the finance minister. You know that I don't know economics. So please come to meet me in Brazil tomorrow. We need you. Well, I was terrified.
<v Edmar Basha>我当时在那所大学的办公室里，也就是天主教大学。我刚上完课没多久就接到了电话。他说，嗯，我刚刚被任命为财政部长。你知道我不懂经济学。所以请明天来巴西见我。我们需要你。嗯，我当时吓坏了。

00:19:39.920 --> 00:20:18.030
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Basha had been waiting for three decades for this call. Ever since he ad his three friends taught graduate school together at the prestigious Catholic university in Rio. Four friends who had been studying Brazilian inflation for a decade. Four buddies at the campus bar complaining to each other about how this government didn't know what it was doing and that government didn't know what it was doing. Four buddies who were now being asked by the government to come and fix thing their way, the plan they'd spent years on. And so, of course, their first answer, no. We don't want to. Here's another one of the four, Andre Lara.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>巴沙为这通电话等了三十年。自从他和他的三个朋友在里约热内卢著名的天主教大学一起教研究生以来一直是如此。这四位朋友研究巴西通货膨胀已经长达十年。四个死党在校园酒吧里互相抱怨这个政府不知道自己在干什么，那个政府也不知道自己在干什么。现在，政府却要求这四个死党按照他们自己的方式来收拾残局，执行他们花了多年心血制定的计划。所以，理所当然地，他们的第一反应是：拒绝。我们不想去。这是四人中的另一位，安德烈·拉拉。

00:20:18.030 --> 00:20:26.950
<v Andre Lara>This is a process. It's something that requires many years. It's not something that we can do. It's not magic. It's not a trick that we do overnight.
<v Andre Lara>这是一个过程。这是一项需要多年时间的工作。这不是我们随手就能做到的。这不是魔法。这不是我们一夜之间就能变出的戏法。

00:20:26.950 --> 00:20:49.280
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>When I asked Lara, wasn't it exciting though? He looked confused for a moment. And then scornful. People, he told me, should be interested in ideas, not feelings. They thought we had a trick. There is no trick. There is only long, hard, complex multi-step macroeconomic plans designed specifically for the Brazilian context.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>当我问拉拉，这难道不是很让人兴奋吗？他迷茫了一会儿。然后露出了不屑的表情。他告诉我，人们应该对理念感兴趣，而不是对情绪感兴趣。他们以为我们有什么戏法。根本没有什么戏法。只有针对巴西国情专门设计的漫长、艰难、复杂的多步骤宏观经济计划。

00:20:49.280 --> 00:21:14.120
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Basha is the more casual of this pair. Yeah, he tells me, we had a trick. But I was busy and I didn't want to move to Brasilia. The government did not give up. Lara and Basha were taken to dinner with members of parliament who told them how much the country needed them. They got calls at home, senators told them, you will have free reign. Whatever you think is best. Basha was invited to meet the president.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在这两人中，巴沙显得更加随性。是的，他告诉我，我们确实有戏法。但我当时很忙，而且我不想搬到巴西利亚去。政府并没有放弃。拉拉和巴沙被邀请与国会议员共进晚餐，议员们向他们倾诉了国家有多么需要他们。他们接到了家里的电话，参议员对他们说，你们将拥有全权。无论你们认为什么最好就行。巴沙还被邀请去见总统。

00:21:14.120 --> 00:21:29.440
<v Edmar Basha>And then I ask an autograph for my kids and then he wrote a note, a note addressed to my two kids in saying, "Please tell your father to work fast for the benefit of the country."
<v Edmar Basha>然后我请求他为我的孩子们签名，接着他写了一张便条，一张写给我两个孩子的便条，上面写着：“请告诉你们的父亲为了国家的利益抓紧工作。”

00:21:29.440 --> 00:21:30.910
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>That's what he wrote in the autograph?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他在签名里写的就是这个？

00:21:30.910 --> 00:21:34.250
<v Edmar Basha>Yes. I still have that note.
<v Edmar Basha>是的。我至今还留着那张便条。

00:21:34.250 --> 00:21:36.060
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So there was a lot of pressure?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以当时压力很大？

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:51.430
<v Edmar Basha>Oh yes. Yes. It's one thing to do it at your office. It is another thing to put the thing together. It had never, never been put in practice anywhere this way.
<v Edmar Basha>哦，是的。是的。在办公室里纸上谈兵是一回事。把东西真正拼凑起来又是另一回事。这种做法在此之前从未在任何地方付诸实践过。

00:21:51.430 --> 00:22:10.680
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Eventually the four signed on and presented their plan. And basically what they said was, you have to hit the underlying causes of inflation. You have to stop the printing presses, stop creating money so quickly. But you also have to stabilize people faith in money. And this is where their plan was different.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>最终这四人签约并提交了他们的计划。基本上他们的意思是，你必须打击通货膨胀的根本原因。你必须停止开动印钞机，停止如此迅速地创造货币。但你同时也必须稳定人们对货币的信心。而这正是他们的计划与众不同的地方。

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:46.280
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>They didn't want to just change the underlying causes of inflation. They wanted to change people themselves. People where the problem. People had to be tricked into thinking money had value, when all signs told them that was absolutely not true. So Basha says, they wrote a plan for a new currency, one that was stable, dependable, trustworthy. The only catch was this currency would not be real. It would not be printed. There would never be coins. It was fake. They called it a virtual currency.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他们不仅仅想改变通货膨胀的根源。他们想要改变人们本身。问题恰恰出在人们身上。必须用计让人们以为货币是有价值的，尽管所有迹象都告诉他们这绝对不是真的。所以巴沙说，他们起草了一份关于新货币的计划，一种稳定、可靠、值得信赖的货币。唯一的条件是，这种货币不会是真实的。它不会被印刷出来。永远不会有硬币。它是假的。他们称之为虚拟货币。

00:22:46.280 --> 00:22:52.690
<v Edmar Basha>We called the unit of real value, URV. Yeah, it was a virtue that didn't exist in fact.
<v Edmar Basha>我们把这个实际价值单位称为URV。是的，它实际上是一个并不存在的虚拟价值。

00:22:52.690 --> 00:23:13.010
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>People would still have cruzeiros, the local currency in their pockets. But when they got paid, their wages would be listed in URVs. Taxes were in URVs and all prices were listed in URVs. And URVs were stable. And so, for example, when you went to the store and bought some milk.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>人们的口袋里依然装着克鲁泽罗，也就是当地的货币。但当他们拿到工资时，他们的薪水将以URV列出。税收以URV计算，所有的价格也都以URV标示。并且URV是稳定的。所以，举个例子，当你去商店买一些牛奶时。

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:35.950
<v Edmar Basha>How much does it cost? Say well, know we have it cost X. Let's say one URV. Well, how much is that because I cannot pay with URV. Well, I have this little table here and today's value of URV in cruzeiros is seven cruzeiros per URV. So it cost one URV, seven cruzeiros.
<v Edmar Basha>它多少钱？店员会说，嗯，现在它值X。比方说一个URV。好吧，这到底是多少钱，因为我不能用URV支付。好吧，我这里有一张小表格，今天URV对克鲁泽罗的汇率是每个URV兑换七个克鲁泽罗。所以它售价一个URV，也就是七个克鲁泽罗。

00:23:35.950 --> 00:23:46.740
<v Edmar Basha>You go next week, well, it's still one URV. But then you say, how many cruzeiros? You look, well, 14.
<v Edmar Basha>你下周再去，嗯，它依然是一个URV。但接着你问，那是多少克鲁泽罗？你看了一下，嗯，十四个。

00:23:46.740 --> 00:24:12.880
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Every night the central bank would put out a memo with the official conversion rate. And a table would get printed in the newspaper. The store clerk could look at the table in the newspaper and see, Monday, one URV is equal to seven cruzeiros. Tuesday, 12 cruzeiros. Wednesday, 14. Milk or whatever it was you were buying would stay the same price. There was no need for the sticker man.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>每天晚上，中央银行都会发布一份带有官方汇率的备忘录。报纸上也会刊登一张表格。店员可以查看报纸上的表格，看到周一时，一个URV等于七个克鲁泽罗。周二，十二个克鲁泽罗。周三，十四个。牛奶或者无论你买的是什么东西，价格都会保持不变。这样就不需要改价员了。

00:24:12.880 --> 00:24:40.580
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And the idea was you would start thinking in URVs. Because just last week you got paid a thousand URVs. Milk costs one URV. Next month, you'd get a thousand URVs again and milk would still be one URV. The exchange into cruzeiros, what you actually handed the clerk would change. But the price in URVs would not. So that was the plan and Basha presented it to the senator from Sao Paulo.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>其核心理念是，你会开始用URV思考。因为就在上周你领到了壹仟URV的工资。牛奶售价一个URV。下个月，你将再次领到壹仟URV，而牛奶依然是一个URV。兑换成克鲁泽罗的金额——也就是你实际递给店员的钱——会发生变化。但以URV计算的价格不会变。这就是整个计划，巴沙把它提交给了圣保罗的参议员。

00:24:40.580 --> 00:25:01.090
<v Edmar Basha>And then when I explained to him the plan, after a while he said, well-- with some anguish in his voice said, well Basha, if this is the only way that you tell me that it can be done, then we will follow you to the precipice.
<v Edmar Basha>然后当我向他解释这个计划时，过了一会儿，他说——他的声音中带着一丝痛苦，说，好吧，巴沙，如果这是你告诉我的唯一可行的方法，那么我们将追随你走向悬崖。

00:25:01.090 --> 00:25:08.940
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And so the four economists went about explaining to the country that everyone should now talk in a virtual currency.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>于是，这四位经济学家开始向全国解释，现在每个人都应该用虚拟货币来交谈。

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:21.200
<v Maria>We didn't understand it what it was. We asked, how much is that? Oh, it's so may URVs. I used to say it was a fantasy because it was not real.
<v Maria>我们当时根本不明白那是什么。我们问，那东西值多少钱？哦，那是多少个URV。我以前常说那是一场幻想，因为它不是真实的。

00:25:21.200 --> 00:25:43.500
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Still, people used it without being forced to. One store would be selling milk for one URV and eventually, all the stores would be. People would know, that's an appropriate price for milk. Which I can tell because I get paid a thousand URVs. Inflation began to go down. 80% a month to 50%. It even got close to 40%.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>即便如此，人们在没有被强迫的情况下还是使用了它。一家商店开始以一个URV的价格出售牛奶，最终所有的商店都这么做了。人们会明白，那是牛奶的合理价格。从我能领到一千URV的工资这一点，我就可以判断出来。通货膨胀开始下降。从每月80%降到50%。甚至接近了40%。

00:25:43.500 --> 00:26:15.440
<v Edmar Basha>And then when we are satisfied that prices are relatively in good synchrony we declare well, from this day, the virtual currency becomes a real currency. The cruzeiro real is going to disappear and everyone is going to receive from now on its wages and pay for all the prices in the new currency, which is the real. Which is equal to one URV. And also, equals to $1. And that is the trick.
<v Edmar Basha>然后，当我们确信价格已经实现了良好的相对同步时，我们宣布，从今天起，虚拟货币将成为真正的货币。克鲁泽罗实将不复存在，从今以后，每个人都将用新货币领取工资并支付所有价格，这种新货币就是雷亚尔。它等于一个URV。同时，也等于1美元。这就是其中的诀窍。

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:40.810
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It wasn't the only trick. While they put the URVs in place, the group of four also made the government balance its budget and slow down on the money creation. And then, one day, July 1, 1994, the central bank deployed truckloads of new cash in this new currency, the real, to banks in the cities, to the provinces, and waited on the ready for the four economists to say go.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这并不是唯一的诀窍。在推行URV的同时，这四人小组还促使政府平衡预算，并放缓了货币创造的速度。接着，在1994年7月1日这一天，中央银行将一车车装有这种新货币——雷亚尔——的现金运送到城市和省份的银行，并随时待命，等待四位经济学家下达出发的指令。

00:26:40.810 --> 00:27:01.840
<v Edmar Basha>And I remember the day that we launched and the real, I have this journalist who had become a friend of mine. And then she came to me and said, Professor, do you swear that inflation's going to end tomorrow. I said, yes, I swear that's going to end tomorrow.
<v Edmar Basha>我还记得我们推出雷亚尔的那天，我有一位已经成为我朋友的记者。然后她来到我面前说，教授，你发誓明天的通货膨胀就会结束吗？我说，是的，我发誓明天它就会结束。

00:27:01.840 --> 00:27:05.270
<v Maria>Everybody was very happy.
<v Maria>每个人都非常高兴。

00:27:05.270 --> 00:27:41.880
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Everyone in Brazil, collectively, as a country, tricked themselves into believing that this fake currency was real. More real than the actual physical bill they were holding in their hands. And that made all the difference. That made it real. For money, it's crazy but that's all you need. People to believe in it. Our four heroes literally turned Brazil's economy in the opposite direction with their plan. Brazil went from being an irrelevant, economic basket-case to one of the most important economies out there. The eighth largest in the world.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>巴西的所有人，作为一个国家，集体在心理上自我欺骗，相信这纸假币是真实的。比他们手里捏着的实际纸币还要真实。而这带来了全部的不同。这让它变成了现实。对于货币来说，这很疯狂，但这就是你所需要的一切。人们对它的信任。我们的四位英雄凭借他们的计划，字面意义上扭转了巴西经济的方向。巴西从一个无关紧要的经济烂摊子一跃成为最重要的经济体之一。全球第八大经济体。

00:27:41.880 --> 00:28:15.560
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Cardoso, the finance minister who hired our four heroes after admitting he knew nothing about economics, was elected president. Twice. And the four economists, despite the fact that most people don't know them by name, really are seen as heroes. These guys made money worth something. Almost everyone I talk to about what happened in 1994 when they got the real described it as magic. Which you'd think the four would like, but Lara does not appreciate decades of research being referred to as magic.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在承认自己对经济学一无所知后依然聘用了这四位英雄的财政部长卡多索，当选为了总统。而且是两次。而这四位经济学家，尽管大多数人并不知道他们的名字，却实实在在被视为英雄。这几个人让货币有了价值。几乎所有跟我聊起1994年他们迎来雷亚尔经历的人，都将其描述为魔术。你可能会觉得这四个人会喜欢这种说法，但拉拉并不欣赏数十年的研究成果被冠以魔术之名。

00:28:15.560 --> 00:28:30.800
<v Andre Lara>There's nothing magical in URV. It's very technical and very-- But basically it was just we established the idea of a unit of account that gives you the sense of value, relative value.
<v Andre Lara>URV里没有任何神奇之处。它非常技术性，而且非常——但基本上我们只是建立了一个记账单位的概念，它赋予你价值感，相对价值。

00:28:30.800 --> 00:28:33.690
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Basha, on the other hand, doesn't mind it so much.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>另一方面，巴沙对这种说法倒并不是很介意。

00:28:33.690 --> 00:28:44.690
<v Edmar Basha>It's very funny because people today, you go around and people think that this is so natural to live in a country without inflation.
<v Edmar Basha>这很有趣，因为今天的人们，你环顾四周，大家会觉得生活在一个没有通货膨胀的国家是如此理所当然的事情。

00:28:44.690 --> 00:29:13.710
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's so natural that now, when you walk into a store in Brazil, instead of a sticker man raising the prices, you can walk out of that sort with your sunglasses in hand and not finish paying for them until six months later. In just 20 years, Brazilians have gone from an absolute faith that their currency has no value to an absolute faith that its value will never change.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>它是如此自然，以至于现在，当你走进巴西的一家商店时，不再会有改价员去抬高价格，你可以手里拿着太阳镜走出那家店，并在半年后才付清尾款。短短20年里，巴西人已经从绝对坚信自己的货币毫无价值，转变为了绝对坚信它的价值永远不会改变。

00:29:13.710 --> 00:29:33.330
<v Ira Glass>Chana Joffe-Walt. Coming up, we go to a place where no journalist has been, this room inside the Federal Reserve that is like financial a black hole where the normal rules of money do not apply. That's in a minute. Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>夏娜·约夫-瓦尔特。接下来，我们将前往一个从未有记者涉足的地方，联邦储备系统内部的这个房间，它就像一个金融黑洞，金钱的常规规则在那里不再适用。广告之后马上回来。芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台，我们的节目将继续播出。

00:29:33.330 --> 00:29:49.380
<v Ira Glass>This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our show of course, we choose a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, "The Invention of Money." We have stories today about the fictional quality of money. How it gets made out of nothing, and what's great about that, and all the trouble that can get us into.
<v Ira Glass>这是美国生活。我是艾拉·格拉斯。当然，每周在我们的节目中，我们都会选择一个主题，为您带来关于该主题的不同类型的故事。今天的节目是“货币的发明”。我们今天讲述的故事关于货币的虚构属性。它是如何凭空产生的，这有什么妙处，以及这会给我们带来多少麻烦。

00:29:49.380 --> 00:29:54.100
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act 2 of our show. Act 2: Weekend at Bernanke's.
<v Ira Glass>我们来到了节目的第二幕。第二幕：伯南克的周末。

00:29:54.100 --> 00:30:41.880
<v Ira Glass>When the United States was on a real gold standard, it was clear what money was and how much money there was. Each dollar corresponded to a dollar of gold that in a vault somewhere. But when we went off the gold standard, somebody had to decide how much money there would be. Some entity had to be entrusted with that power. In our country and just about every modern country today, we give that power to something called a central bank. Our central bank is named the Federal Reserve. You've heard that name, probably. And like all central banks, it has one magical power. The Federal Reserve can create money any time it wants. It's the one institution in America that can decide, OK, the economy would run better, interest rates would drop, whatever, if there was more money out there. Or it can decide there should be less money. It controls the amount of money in our economy, including in a very literal way.
<v Ira Glass>当美国采用真正的金本位时，货币是什么、有多少货币是一目了然的。每一美元都对应着某个金库里的一美元黄金。但当我们放弃金本位后，必须有人来决定应该有多少货币。某个实体必须被赋予这种权力。在我们国家和当今几乎所有现代国家，我们将这种权力交给了所谓的中央银行。我们的中央银行名为美联储。你可能听过这个名字。和所有中央银行一样，它拥有一种神奇的权力。美联储随时可以根据需要创造货币。它是美国唯一一家能够决定“好，如果有更多的货币在流通，经济就会运转得更好，利率就会下降，诸如此类”的机构。或者它可以决定应该减少货币。它控制着我们经济中的货币总量，而且是以一种非常字面的方式。

00:30:41.880 --> 00:30:55.120
<v Ira Glass>If you have any cash in your pocket right now, pull it out, you see the name right at the top of the bills, Federal Reserve note. They're the ones who call up the Bureau of Engraving and Printing and say, print some more of the stuff. And here's where things get tricky.
<v Ira Glass>如果你现在口袋里有任何现金，把它掏出来，你会看到纸币正上方的名字：联邦储备券（Federal Reserve note）。正是他们打电话给雕版印刷局（Bureau of Engraving and Printing）说，再印点这东西。而问题就在这里变得棘手起来。

00:30:55.120 --> 00:31:23.380
<v Ira Glass>Though the Federal Reserve does that, and though the Federal Reserve's chairman is appointed by the president. And though the Federal Reserve is subject to congressional oversight, and though the Federal Reserve's name includes the word, "federal," it is not actually part the federal government. It's an independent institution. And you may remember presidents like Nixon wanting the Fed to do this or that and the Fed chairman basically telling them to go take a hike. They're supposed to do what they think is best for the economy, not what's best for the president's political future.
<v Ira Glass>尽管美联储在这样做，尽管美联储的主席是由总统任命的，尽管美联储受国会监督，而且尽管美联储的名字里包含了“联邦”一词，但它实际上并不是联邦政府的一部分。它是一个独立的机构。你可能还记得像尼克松这样的总统希望美联储这样做或那样做，而美联储主席基本上叫他们走开。他们应该做他们认为对经济最有利的事情，而不是对总统的政治前途最有利的事情。

00:31:23.380 --> 00:31:26.370
<v Ira Glass>With me so far?
<v Ira Glass>到现在为止能跟上吗？

00:31:26.370 --> 00:32:02.600
<v Ira Glass>Since 2008 when the current financial crisis took hold, the Fed has done all kinds of things that central banks just don't do. Stuff the Fed has never done. Stuff that would have been unthinkable in the past. It's all happening on a scale that is bigger than anything the Fed has ever tried in its history. Basically, the Fed has gone to central bank crazytown. And the danger, if the Fed screws up is that the fiction that we call the US dollar loses value. The people stop believing in it. Alex Blumberg and David Kestenbaum are to explain to us now two things, what the Fed usually does and what it's done since 2008 that seems so extreme.
<v Ira Glass>自2008年当前金融危机爆发以来，美联储做了各种中央银行通常不会做的事情。美联储从未做过的事情。过去简直不可想象的事情。这一切的发生规模之大，超越了美联储历史上尝试过的任何事情。基本上，美联储已经进入了中央银行的疯狂小镇。而如果美联储搞砸了，危险在于我们称之为美元的虚构概念将失去价值。人们不再相信它。亚历克斯·布隆伯格（Alex Blumberg）和大卫·克斯滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum）现在要向我们解释两件事：美联储通常做什么，以及自2008年以来它做了什么显得如此极端的事。

00:32:02.600 --> 00:32:12.080
<v Alex Blumberg>Up until recently when the Federal Reserve did this dangerous and magical thing of creating money out of nothing, conjuring money out of the void, it happened in a kind of solemn ritual.
<v Alex Blumberg>直到最近，当美联储做出这种用虚无创造货币的危险而神奇的事情、凭空变出货币时，它是在一种庄严的仪式中进行的。

00:32:12.080 --> 00:32:19.280
<v David Kestenbaum>The ritual goes like this, every six weeks in an ornate conference room inside the Federal Reserve building in Washington, DC, there's a meeting.
<v David Kestenbaum>这个仪式的过程是这样的：每隔六周，在华盛顿特区美联储大楼内一个装饰华丽的会议室里，会举行一次会议。

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:32.890
<v Alex Blumberg>The meeting is closed to the press, members of Congress, even the president isn't allowed. The meeting is run by the chairman of the Fed, currently Ben Bernanke. You've heard his name in the news a lot. It is a meeting of the Federal Reserve's Open Market Committee.
<v Alex Blumberg>这次会议不对媒体、国会议员开放，甚至连总统也不允许参加。会议由美联储主席主持，现任主席是本·伯南克。你经常在新闻中听到他的名字。这是美联储公开市场委员会（Federal Reserve's Open Market Committee）的会议。

00:32:32.890 --> 00:32:43.080
<v Gerald O'driscoll>Even though major things, major policy issues are being decided, it's not an exciting experience.
<v Gerald O'driscoll>尽管正在决定重大事情、重大政策问题，但这并不是一次令人兴奋的经历。

00:32:43.080 --> 00:32:50.730
<v Alex Blumberg>This is Gerald O'Driscoll, Jr. who attended a couple of these meetings back in the 1990s when he was vice president of the Dallas Regional Federal Reserve.
<v Alex Blumberg>这是杰拉尔德·奥德里斯科尔（Gerald O'Driscoll, Jr.），他在20世纪90年代担任达拉斯地区美联储副行长期间参加过几次这样的会议。

00:32:50.730 --> 00:33:07.610
<v David Kestenbaum>So at these meetings, all these Federal Reserve officials get up and make presentations about the economy. What are the bright spots? what are the weak spots? Are things getting better, worse, staying about the same? And then, they take a vote to decide the big question. Should there be more or less money in the US economy?
<v David Kestenbaum>所以在这些会议上，所有这些美联储官员都起身作关于经济的报告。亮点是什么？薄弱环节是什么？情况是在变好、变坏，还是大致保持不变？然后，他们进行投票来决定那个重大问题：美国经济中的货币应该增加还是减少？

00:33:07.610 --> 00:33:22.020
<v Alex Blumberg>Now this question has huge ramifications. More money generally means it's easier for businesses and everyday people to get loans, which means it's easier for businesses to expand or new businesses to start up. Which ultimately translates into more jobs and a better economy.
<v Alex Blumberg>现在，这个问题有着巨大的影响。更多的货币通常意味着企业和普通人更容易获得贷款，这意味着企业更容易扩张或新企业更容易创办。这最终转化为更多的就业机会和更好的经济。

00:33:22.020 --> 00:33:30.060
<v David Kestenbaum>But, more money can also mean inflation. Which if it gets out of control, can really cripple the economy. Just ask the people of Brazil.
<v David Kestenbaum>但是，更多的货币也可能意味着通货膨胀。如果失控，通货膨胀真的会瘫痪经济。去问问巴西人民就知道了。

00:33:30.060 --> 00:33:38.920
<v Alex Blumberg>So it is a balancing act, with the fate of the US economy hinging on the Fed's decision. Not that anyone there acts that way. Again, Gerald O'Driscoll.
<v Alex Blumberg>所以这是一次平衡壮举，美国经济的命运悬于美联储的决定之上。并不是说那里的任何人表现得好像是这样。再次请出杰拉尔德·奥德里斯科尔。

00:33:38.920 --> 00:33:49.560
<v Gerald O'driscoll>It's almost ceremonial. There's an order in which the staff reports take place. The questioning is kind of orchestrated.
<v Gerald O'driscoll>这几乎是礼仪性的。工作人员汇报的顺序是有规定的。提问过程有点像排练好的。

00:33:49.560 --> 00:33:54.130
<v Alex Blumberg>They're only like, we're pulling the levers of the largest economy in the history of mankind.
<v Alex Blumberg>他们只是像是在说，我们在拉动人类历史上面积最大的经济体的杠杆。

00:33:54.130 --> 00:34:04.780
<v Gerald O'driscoll>Yeah. You almost forget that that's what's happening until they start to read the policy directive, which is exactly what they're going to do. Then you remind yourself what all this has been about.
<v Gerald O'driscoll>是的。你几乎会忘记正在发生的事情，直到他们开始宣读政策指令——也就是他们具体要做的事情。然后你会提醒自己这一切究竟是为了什么。

00:34:04.780 --> 00:34:19.710
<v Alex Blumberg>I sort of think of it like a joystick. You move it too far in one direction, you get out of control inflation. You move it too far in the other direction and then you can really sort of put the brakes on the economy. Is that too simplistic a way of thinking about it or is that?
<v Alex Blumberg>我有点把它想象成一个操纵杆。你把它往一个方向推得太远，就会引发失控的通胀。你把它往另一个方向推得太远，然后你真的会给经济踩急刹车。这种想法是不是太简单化了，还是说？

00:34:19.710 --> 00:34:31.270
<v Gerald O'driscoll>I mean it's OK to think about it that way. I winced a little when you said that because the joystick presumes a very precise control, which is exactly what they don't have.
<v Gerald O'driscoll>我的意思是，这样想是可以的。你这么说时我有点畏缩，因为操纵杆假设了一种非常精确的控制，而这正是他们所没有的。

00:34:31.270 --> 00:34:33.250
<v Alex Blumberg>Right.
<v Alex Blumberg>对。

00:34:33.250 --> 00:34:42.890
<v Gerald O'driscoll>It's more like you're moving a super tanker and you start moving the wheel and there's no effect that you can see for quite a while.
<v Gerald O'driscoll>这更像是你在移动一艘超级油轮，你开始转动方向盘，但在相当长的一段时间里你看不到任何效果。

00:34:42.890 --> 00:34:59.400
<v David Kestenbaum>OK, so once they've decided to move the super tanker, how exactly do they create money out of nothing? Well the answer, the mechanics of how this works, are so weird and bizarre that when I first learned about it, I thought wait, that's what this all comes down to?
<v David Kestenbaum>好的，一旦他们决定移动这艘超级油轮，他们究竟是如何凭空创造货币的？答案是，其运作机制是如此诡异和奇特，以至于我第一次了解到的时候，我想：等等，这就是这一切的归宿吗？

00:34:59.400 --> 00:35:17.520
<v Alex Blumberg>They can't just create a whole bunch of money and then walk out to the street corner and start handing it out to people. That would be awkward for one, and it also would take too long. They need to get large amounts of money into the economy fast. And so they need someone who can handle a few billion dollars in one quick transaction, which means the banking system. Banks.
<v Alex Blumberg>他们不能只是凭空造出一大堆钱，然后走到街角开始分发给人们。首先这很尴尬，而且也太花时间了。他们需要迅速将巨额资金注入经济。因此，他们需要一个能够在一次快速交易中处理数十亿美元的人，这意味着银行系统。银行。

00:35:17.520 --> 00:35:21.850
<v Alex Blumberg>And how do they get money into the banks hands? They buy something from them.
<v Alex Blumberg>他们是如何把钱弄到银行手里的呢？他们从银行买点东西。

00:35:21.850 --> 00:35:34.500
<v David Kestenbaum>The most boring, safest, surest thing any bank owns, bonds issued by the US government. Treasury bonds. Big banks typically have billions of dollars in treasury bonds just sitting around on their books.
<v David Kestenbaum>任何银行拥有的最无聊、最安全、最确定的东西：美国政府发行的债券。国债。大银行通常在账面上闲置着数十亿美元的国债。

00:35:34.500 --> 00:35:52.380
<v Alex Blumberg>And to buy these bonds, the Fed doesn't show up at the door with an armored car full of cash. Don't picture that. You need to picture a computer screen at the Fed that shows the amount of money in a bank's account. Richard Dzina is a Senior Vice President of the New York Fed and he says, "someone at the Fed just changes that number. Adds a few billion, clicks a mouse--
<v Alex Blumberg>为了购买这些债券，美联储并没有带着装满现金的装甲车出现在门前。不要那样想象。你需要想象美联储的一个电脑屏幕，上面显示着银行账户里的金额。理查德·兹纳（Richard Dzina）是纽约联邦储备银行的高级副总裁，他说：“美联储的某个人只需更改那个数字。增加几十亿，点击一下鼠标——”

00:35:52.380 --> 00:35:54.700
<v Richard Dzina>And voila, money is created.
<v Richard Dzina>然后，瞧，货币就被创造出来了。

00:35:54.700 --> 00:35:59.040
<v David Kestenbaum>Basically you press a button and in their account it says we have this much more money?
<v David Kestenbaum>基本上你按一个按钮，他们的账户上就显示我们多了这么多钱？

00:35:59.040 --> 00:36:00.030
<v Richard Dzina>Yes.
<v Richard Dzina>是的。

00:36:00.030 --> 00:36:02.660
<v David Kestenbaum>Does that seem weird to you at all after all these years?
<v David Kestenbaum>经历了这么多年的，你还觉得这很奇怪吗？

00:36:02.660 --> 00:36:12.230
<v Richard Dzina>Yes, it is still a magical process.
<v Richard Dzina>是的，这仍然是一个神奇的过程。

00:36:12.230 --> 00:36:25.600
<v Alex Blumberg>All right, so the bank had a million dollars worth of bonds and now it has a million in cash. What's the big deal? A billion's a billion, right? But banks don't like to sit on lots of cash. They went to lend it out and earn interest on it. That's what banks do.
<v Alex Blumberg>好吧，所以这家银行有一百万美元的债券，现在它有了一百万现金。有什么大不了的？十亿就是十亿，对吧？但银行不喜欢坐拥大量现金。他们想把钱贷出去并从中赚取利息。这就是银行的业务。

00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:42.690
<v David Kestenbaum>So the banks start landing that money out. And that is how this new money enters into the economy. So if I'm a bank, before maybe I'd loan my money out at 6%. Now I got more money sitting around. I want to move it out the door fast, so I'll loan it out at a lower rate. Maybe 4%.
<v David Kestenbaum>于是银行开始把钱贷出去。这就是这些新钱进入经济的方式。所以如果我是一家银行，以前我可能会以6%的利率把钱贷出去。现在我有更多的闲置资金。我想快点把它推销出去，所以我以较低的利率把它贷出去。也许是4%。

00:36:42.690 --> 00:36:53.470
<v Alex Blumberg>And this is why you're always hearing on the news the Fed lowered interest rates or raised interest rates. They did that through this process we just described. Creating money out of nothing, buying or selling treasury bonds.
<v Alex Blumberg>这就是为什么你总是在新闻里听到美联储降息或加息的原因。他们是通过我们刚刚描述的过程做到的。凭空创造货币，买卖国债。

00:36:53.470 --> 00:37:04.980
<v David Kestenbaum>OK, so that's what the Feds did for decades. The boring, sober, purchase or sale of boring, safe bonds. Now to Fe Crazytown.
<v David Kestenbaum>好的，这就是美联储几十年来一直在做的事情。无聊、稳健地买卖无聊、安全的债券。现在进入美联储疯狂小镇。

00:37:04.980 --> 00:37:23.350
<v Alex Blumberg>In the financial crisis. Starting in 2007, banks and firms up and down Wall Street were looking shaky. They had all these assets that were plunging in value and they needed someone to lend them money to offset their losses and stay afloat. And it was around this time that Jim Cramer freaked out on television.
<v Alex Blumberg>在金融危机期间。从2007年开始，华尔街上下的大银行和公司看起来都摇摇欲坠。它们的所有这些资产价值都在暴跌，它们需要有人借钱给它们以抵消损失并维持运营。正是在这个时候，吉姆·克莱默（Jim Cramer）在电视上崩溃了。

00:37:23.350 --> 00:37:37.260
<v Alex Blumberg>Jim Cramer you may know, is the host of Mad Money on CNBC. And here is in August 2007, the very beginning of the subprime collapse talking about the Federal Reserve chairman Ben Bernanke. Actually, talking is probably the wrong word.
<v Alex Blumberg>你可能知道，吉姆·克莱默是CNBC节目《疯狂金钱》（Mad Money）的主持人。这是他在2007年8月，也就是次贷崩溃的初期，谈论美联储主席本·伯南克。事实上，用“谈论”这个词可能不太准确。

00:37:37.260 --> 00:37:45.060
<v Jim Cramer>Bernanke is being an academic. He has no idea how bad it is out there. He has no idea! He has no idea!
<v Jim Cramer>伯南克是个学者。他根本不知道外面情况有多糟。他一无所知！他一无所知！

00:37:45.060 --> 00:37:45.930
<v Woman>Cramer.
<v Woman>克莱默。

00:37:45.930 --> 00:38:02.980
<v Jim Cramer>I have talked to heads of almost every single one of these firms in the last 72 hours and he has no idea what it's like out there. None! My people have been in this game for 25 years and they are losing their jobs, and these firms are going to go out of business. And he's nuts!. They're nuts!. They know nothing!
<v Jim Cramer>在过去的72小时里，我与几乎所有这些公司的负责人交谈过，他根本不知道外面的情况是什么样的。一点也不知道！我的人在这个行业干了25年，他们正在失去工作，而这些公司即将倒闭。他疯了！他们疯了！他们什么都不懂！

00:38:02.980 --> 00:38:04.830
<v Woman>Cramer.
<v Woman>克莱默。

00:38:04.830 --> 00:38:09.740
<v Jim Cramer>This is a different kind of market and the Fed is asleep.
<v Jim Cramer>这是一种不同类型的市场，而美联储在睡大觉。

00:38:09.740 --> 00:38:38.070
<v Alex Blumberg>So what Jim Cramer is saying in this very entertaining freak out is that the Fed should use its power to create money out of nothing in a way that we haven't talked about yet. In addition to the ceremony meetings the Fed has every six weeks about the money supply, it also has these special emergency powers that it's only supposed to use in situations where the banking system is on the verge of collapse. And Cramer here is asking the Fed to use those powers to become what's called, "the lender of last resort."
<v Alex Blumberg>所以吉姆·克莱默在这场极其滑稽的精神崩溃中想要表达的是，美联储应该以我们尚未讨论过的方式，利用其权力凭空创造货币。除了每六周关于货币供应量的例行会议外，美联储还拥有这些特殊的紧急权力，这些权力本应仅在银行系统处于崩溃边缘的情况下使用。克莱默在这里要求美联储利用这些权力成为所谓的“最后贷款人”。

00:38:38.070 --> 00:38:39.980
<v Alex Blumberg>Specifically, he said this.
<v Alex Blumberg>具体来说，他是这么说的。

00:38:39.980 --> 00:38:43.600
<v Jim Cramer>Listen, open the darn Fed window.
<v Jim Cramer>听着，打开该死的美联储窗口。

00:38:43.600 --> 00:38:45.480
<v David Kestenbaum>The darn Fed window.
<v David Kestenbaum>该死的美联储窗口。

00:38:45.480 --> 00:38:59.400
<v David Kestenbaum>The Fed window is jargon for the Fed creating a bunch of money out of nothing and lending it to institutions in crisis who can't borrow money through the normal private market. Sort of like a bank window where you could walk up and get an emergency loan from a magical genie.
<v David Kestenbaum>美联储窗口是行话，指的是美联储凭空创造出一大堆钱，并将其借给无法通过正常私人市场借到钱的危机中的机构。有点像一个银行窗口，你可以走过去从一个神奇的精灵那里获得紧急贷款。

00:38:59.400 --> 00:39:19.600
<v Alex Blumberg>This was a revolutionary proposal on several levels. A lot of these financial institutions that needed help were not traditional banks. And the Fed hadn't lent emergency money to something other than a boring, highly regulated bank for over half a century. But seven months after Cramer's tirade, though certainly not because of it, the Fed did just what he was suggesting.
<v Alex Blumberg>这是一个在几个层面上都具有革命性的提议。许多需要帮助的金融机构并不是传统的银行。而且半个多世纪以来，美联储一直没有向无聊、高度监管的银行以外的机构出借紧急资金。但在克莱默发表长篇大论七个月后，当然肯定不是因为他的缘故，美联储做了他所建议的事情。

00:39:19.600 --> 00:39:31.120
<v Reporter>A dramatic story has broken this morning to the effect that JP Morgan and the New York Fed are combining to in essence, bail out Bear Stearns.
<v Reporter>今天早上爆出一个戏剧性的消息，摩根大通和纽约联储正在联合起来，实质上是营救贝尔斯登（Bear Stearns）。

00:39:31.120 --> 00:39:51.890
<v David Kestenbaum>As the financial crisis unfolded, the Fed created over one trillion dollars. Which it lent out as emergency loans to all the big names on Wall Street: Goldman Sachs, Morgan Stanley, huge banks like City Group and Bank of America. The Fed lent money to private equity firms, hedge funds, and even regular companies like Verizon, GE, and Harley Davidson.
<v David Kestenbaum>随着金融危机的展开，美联储创造了超过一万亿美元。它将其作为紧急贷款出借给华尔街的所有大机构：高盛、摩根士丹利、花旗集团和美国银行等大银行。美联储甚至将资金借给私募股权公司、对冲基金，甚至是像威瑞森（Verizon）、通用电气（GE）和哈雷戴维森（Harley Davidson）这样的一般公司。

00:39:51.890 --> 00:39:59.540
<v Alex Blumberg>And it wasn't just the recipients of that cash that were new, it was also what the Fed was requiring in return, the collateral.
<v Alex Blumberg>而且不只是获得现金的对象是新的，美联储所要求的抵押品也是新的。

00:39:59.540 --> 00:40:11.610
<v Alex Blumberg>In the past, in the rare instances that the Fed used its powers to serve as the lender of last resort, it demanded the highest quality collateral in return. Assets that were safe and would hold their value.
<v Alex Blumberg>过去，在美联储罕见地行使其权力充当最后贷款人的情况下，它要求获得最高质量的抵押品作为回报。那些安全且能保值的资产。

00:40:11.610 --> 00:40:28.220
<v David Kestenbaum>In other words, the Fed treated banks in financial trouble the same way a hard-nosed pawn broker treats people in financial trouble. It would hold onto your grandmother's antique earrings and in return, give you far less than they're worth. And if you bring in cheap plastic earrings, the Fed wouldn't give you any money at all.
<v David Kestenbaum>换句话说，美联储对待陷入财务困境的银行的方式，就像一个冷酷无情的当铺对待陷入财务困境的人一样。它会扣押你祖母的古董耳环，作为回报，给你的钱远低于它们的实际价值。如果你拿来廉价的塑料耳环，美联储根本不会给你任何钱。

00:40:28.220 --> 00:40:35.390
<v Alex Blumberg>But in 2008, the Fed started accepting all sorts of cheap, plastic jewelry. Collateral that just months ago it never would have touched.
<v Alex Blumberg>但在2008年，美联储开始接受各种廉价的塑料首饰。这些抵押品在几个月前它是绝对不会碰的。

00:40:35.390 --> 00:40:55.610
<v David Kestenbaum>For example, at one point in the crisis, the Fed acquired $75 billion worth of toxic assets from Bear Stearns and AIG. Our colleague Chana Joffe-Walt talked to Vincent Reinhart, another former Fed official now at the American Enterprise Institute about just how disturbingly unboring these assets were. There were mortgages on resort hotels.
<v David Kestenbaum>例如，在危机的某个阶段，美联储从贝尔斯登和AIG收购了价值750亿美元的有毒资产。我们的同事查娜·约夫-瓦尔特（Chana Joffe-Walt）与另一位前美联储官员、现就职于美国企业研究所（American Enterprise Institute）的文森特·莱因哈特（Vincent Reinhart）交谈，谈到了这些资产是多么令人不安地不无聊。那里有度假村酒店的抵押贷款。

00:40:55.610 --> 00:40:58.570
<v Vincent Reinhart>It's a grab bag. There's a lot of Hilton properties.
<v Vincent Reinhart>这是一个大杂烩。有很多希尔顿的物业。

00:40:58.570 --> 00:41:01.480
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The Hilton Hawaiian Village, the Hilton of Puerto Rico.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>夏威夷希尔顿度假村，波多黎各希尔顿。

00:41:01.480 --> 00:41:04.170
<v Vincent Reinhart>Yeah. The Maldives by the way.
<v Vincent Reinhart>是的。顺便说一句，还有马尔代夫的。

00:41:04.170 --> 00:41:05.830
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The Maldives are on here, yeah.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>马尔代夫的也在上面，对。

00:41:05.830 --> 00:41:09.710
<v Vincent Reinhart>The Fed has a claim on a hotel in Jacksonville, Florida.
<v Vincent Reinhart>美联储对佛罗里达州杰克逊维尔的一家酒店拥有索赔权。

00:41:09.710 --> 00:41:16.110
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So there's a mall in this portfolio that I believe has gone into bankruptcy.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以这个投资组合里还有一个购物中心，我相信它已经破产了。

00:41:16.110 --> 00:41:16.940
<v Vincent Reinhart>What's the name of it?
<v Vincent Reinhart>叫什么名字？

00:41:16.940 --> 00:41:19.710
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Crossroads.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>十字路口（Crossroads）。

00:41:19.710 --> 00:41:28.570
<v Vincent Reinhart>Crossroads? Crossroads Mall in Oklahoma actually, isn't it? Oh, yeah, it owns it. REO means real estate owned.
<v Vincent Reinhart>十字路口？其实是俄克拉荷马州的十字路口购物中心（Crossroads Mall），对吧？哦，是的，它拥有这个。REO意思是拥有的房地产。

00:41:28.570 --> 00:41:33.830
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So the Fed owns that mall? So the Fed is now in the commercial shopping business?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以美联储拥有那个购物中心？所以美联储现在涉足商业购物行业了？

00:41:33.830 --> 00:41:36.440
<v Vincent Reinhart>Yeah. It's mind-bending, isn't it?
<v Vincent Reinhart>是的。这太颠覆三观了，不是吗？

00:41:36.440 --> 00:41:38.260
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It is a little mind-bending.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>确实有点颠覆三观。

00:41:38.260 --> 00:42:01.480
<v Vincent Reinhart>So actually, you should have the image of chairman Ben Bernanke flying to a speaking engagement and he could look out the window and look down and say, boy, I own a piece of that. I own a piece of that. I own a piece of that. And that's the way the Fed's balance sheet is right now.
<v Vincent Reinhart>所以实际上，你应该想象这样一幅画面：主席本·伯南克坐飞机去参加演讲，他可以朝窗外往下看，然后说：“天哪，我拥有那里的一个份额。我拥有那里的一个份额。我拥有那里的一个份额。”这就是美联储目前的资产负债表状况。

00:42:01.480 --> 00:42:03.030
<v Alex Blumberg>And it didn't stop there.
<v Alex Blumberg>而且它并没有就此停止。

00:42:03.030 --> 00:42:14.890
<v Alex Blumberg>In late 2008, the economy was still in trouble, so the Fed launched another program. Which would become by far, the biggest single purchase they'd ever made. They would be creating more money than ever before.
<v Alex Blumberg>在2008年底，经济仍然处于困境中，于是美联储推出了另一个项目。这将成为迄今为止他们进行过的最大规模的单笔收购。他们将创造比以往更多的货币。

00:42:14.890 --> 00:42:42.830
<v David Kestenbaum>But what were they going to buy with that money to get the money out into the economy? Well, what is there out there that adds up to hundreds of billions of dollars? Home mortgages. The Fed would buy home mortgages. Not the sub prime ones that got into trouble. This was not a plan to rid the banks of bad assets. They would be buying safe, high quality mortgages. But they would be buying a lot of them. In the form of mortgage backed securities. The hope was this would inject a lot of new money into the economy and prop us the housing market.
<v David Kestenbaum>但他们打算用这些钱买什么，从而把钱注入实体经济呢？那么，有什么东西能累积到数千亿美元规模呢？房屋抵押贷款。美联储将购买房屋抵押贷款。不是那种出问题的次级贷款。这不是一项帮银行剥离不良资产的计划。他们买的是安全、高质量的抵押贷款。但他们会买很多。以抵押贷款支持证券的形式。当时的希望是，这能向实体经济注入大量新资金，并支撑房地产市场。

00:42:42.830 --> 00:42:54.310
<v Alex Blumberg>But how many to buy, that was the question. Vincent Reinhart, the former Fed official, says that the Fed had no idea how many mortgages it would need to purchase to do all this. How much money should it create?
<v Alex Blumberg>但究竟要买多少，这才是问题所在。前美联储官员文森特·莱inhart（Vincent Reinhart）表示，美联储根本不知道为了完成这一切需要购买多少抵押贷款。它应该创造多少货币？

00:42:54.310 --> 00:43:12.230
<v Vincent Reinhart>They were working in the dark. Economic theory doesn't tell you how many securities you have to buy to have a predictable effect on interest rates or activity. So they went and they picked a round number. A big, round number.
<v Vincent Reinhart>他们是在黑暗中摸索。经济学理论并没有告诉你，你必须购买多少证券，才能对利率或经济活动产生可预测的影响。所以他们去挑选了一个整数字。一个巨大、整齐的数字。

00:43:12.230 --> 00:43:18.320
<v Alex Blumberg>Do you imagine them actually sitting around saying, I don't know, a hundred billion, two hundred, a trillion? 700? How about 5?
<v Alex Blumberg>你能想象他们当时实际上坐在那里说，我不知道，一千亿、两千亿、一万亿？七千亿？五千亿怎么样？

00:43:18.320 --> 00:43:32.570
<v Vincent Reinhart>I can imagine them sitting around in that big room around a big table arguing in terms of probably quarters of trillions. How about 250? No, how about 500?
<v Vincent Reinhart>我可以想象他们坐在那个大房间里，围着一张大桌子，可能以四分之一万亿为单位争论着。2500亿怎么样？不，5000亿怎么样？

00:43:32.570 --> 00:43:37.880
<v Alex Blumberg>In the end, the number they settled on was massive, one and a quarter trillion dollars.
<v Alex Blumberg>最后，他们确定的数字是庞大的——一万二千五百亿美元。

00:43:37.880 --> 00:43:50.380
<v David Kestenbaum>Just a reminder, a trillion dollars is to a million dollars what a million dollars is to $1.
<v David Kestenbaum>提醒一下，一万亿美元对一百万美元的关系，就相当于一百万美元对1美元的关系。

00:43:50.380 --> 00:44:25.780
<v David Kestenbaum>For some reason that's hard for me to explain. I wanted to see the room where this happened, where the money gets made. It seemed like a long shot, the Fed is historically very media shy. It doesn't grant on the record interviews, much less tours. But this time the Fed said OK. And so one morning, Chana Joffe-Walt and I found ourselves at the southern tip of Manhattan, outside the Federal Reserve Bank of New York. It's a big, imposing building. The iron work, the marble in the lobby all say, I'm a big, imposing building. I am the central bank of the biggest economy in the history of the world. They've still got a bunch of gold in the basement. And by bunch, I mean possibly the largest collection of gold anywhere.
<v David Kestenbaum>出于某种我很难解释的原因，我很想看看发生这一切的房间，那个印钱的地方。这似乎是个奢望，美联储历史上一直非常羞于面对媒体。它不接受正式采访，更不用说参观了。但这一次，美联储说行。于是，一天早晨，查娜·乔夫-沃尔特（Chana Joffe-Walt）和我发现自己身处曼哈顿南端，在纽约联邦储备银行外面。这是一座宏伟、庄严的建筑。铁艺、大厅里的大理石都在诉说着：我是一座宏伟、庄严的建筑。我是世界历史上最大经济体的中央银行。他们的地下室里仍然存着一大堆黄金。我说的一大堆，指的是可能是任何地方规模最大的黄金收藏。

00:44:25.780 --> 00:44:28.770
<v David Kestenbaum>And then, our guide, Nate Wuerffel, took us up to the room.
<v David Kestenbaum>然后，我们的向导内特·韦尔费尔（Nate Wuerffel）带我们走进了那个房间。

00:44:28.770 --> 00:44:40.580
<v Nate Wuerffel>So this is the mortgage backed security purchase program team room. It's a small-ish room. It's not as big as you might think when you consider the size of the overall purchase program.
<v Nate Wuerffel>所以这就是抵押贷款支持证券购买计划团队办公室。这是一个相对较小的房间。考虑到整体购买计划的规模，它可能没有你想象的那么大。

00:44:40.580 --> 00:45:04.930
<v David Kestenbaum>Whatever you imagine the room looks like where you can create one and a quarter trillion dollars, this is not it. It is not grand. It is not ornate. It is not ceremonial. It has four grey cubicles, it has computer screens, and there's no other way to say this: it's a mess. There are papers and notes scattered around. There is a yoga ball someone has been sitting on. And there is a basketball net, possibly Nerf brand. This is where the magic happens.
<v David Kestenbaum>无论你想象中能创造一万二千五百亿美元的房间长什么样，都不会是这个样子。它一点也不宏伟。一点也不富丽堂皇。一点也不讲究仪式感。它有四个灰色的隔间，有电脑屏幕，而且只能这么说：它简直一团糟。到处散落着纸张和笔记。有一个别人坐过的瑜伽球。还有一个可能是Nerf品牌的篮球架。这就是奇迹发生的地方。

00:45:04.930 --> 00:45:11.330
<v David Kestenbaum>Julie Remache and her colleagues spent weeks and weeks and weeks in this room, buying and buying and buying.
<v David Kestenbaum>朱莉·雷马什（Julie Remache）和她的同事们在这个房间里度过了整整好几周的时间，不断地买进、买进、再买进。

00:45:11.330 --> 00:45:22.760
<v Julie Remache>This program was big. It represents 20% of the outstanding agency mortgage backed securities in the market. So it's big.
<v Julie Remache>这个计划规模很大。它占了市场上未偿机构抵押贷款支持证券的20%。所以它很大。

00:45:22.760 --> 00:45:24.710
<v David Kestenbaum>You bought a fifth of what was out there?
<v David Kestenbaum>你们买下了市场上五分之一的份额？

00:45:24.710 --> 00:45:26.150
<v Julie Remache>That's right.
<v Julie Remache>没错。

00:45:26.150 --> 00:45:33.430
<v David Kestenbaum>Can I ask a kind of weird question, but I know it's one everyone's going to ask. Where does the money come from?
<v David Kestenbaum>我能问一个有点奇怪的问题吗，但我知道这也是每个人都会问的问题。钱是从哪里来的？

00:45:33.430 --> 00:45:52.750
<v Nate Wuerffel>It's created. So that's the nature of central banking. So central banks, when we actually purchase a security, we pay for it with cash that is basically created by the central bank. That's the nature of central banking around the world.
<v Nate Wuerffel>是创造出来的。这就是中央银行的本质。因此，当中央银行实际购买证券时，我们用基本上由中央银行创造的现金来支付。这就是世界各地中央银行的本质。

00:45:52.750 --> 00:46:11.340
<v Alex Blumberg>The sheer amount of new money that the Fed created was unprecedented. From the time we went off the gold standard of 1933 until 2008, the Fed had created a net total of $800 billion. In the months after the financial crisis, that number nearly tripled to almost $2.4 trillion.
<v Alex Blumberg>美联储创造的新货币绝对数量是空前的。从1933年我们放弃金本位制到2008年，美联储创造的净总额为8000亿美元。在金融危机爆发后的几个月里，这个数字几乎翻了两番，达到近2.4万亿美元。

00:46:11.340 --> 00:46:26.190
<v Alex Blumberg>Julie and her colleagues were spending more newly created money in just 15 months than the Fed had created in its entire history up until 2008.
<v Alex Blumberg>朱莉和她的同事们在短短15个月里花掉的新创造资金，比美联储自成立到2008年整个历史时期创造的还要多。

00:46:26.190 --> 00:46:27.190
<v Ira Glass>Hey fellas.
<v Ira Glass>嘿，伙计们。

00:46:27.190 --> 00:46:27.720
<v Alex Blumberg>Hey Ira.
<v Alex Blumberg>嘿，艾拉。

00:46:27.720 --> 00:46:30.750
<v David Kestenbaum>Hey, what are you doing here, we're doing a radio story.
<v David Kestenbaum>嘿，你们在这里干什么，我们在做一个广播节目。

00:46:30.750 --> 00:46:46.710
<v Ira Glass>Well I know. Actually, I've been listening and it's fascinating of course. And excellent story as always. But I have some questions. For starters, you're saying that the Fed created over a trillion dollars to bailout the economy. Does that money get added to the deficit?
<v Ira Glass>我当然知道。其实我一直在听，这当然很引人入胜。而且一如既往是个精彩的故事。但我有一些问题。首先，你们说美联储创造了超过一万亿美元来拯救经济。这些钱会被计入赤字吗？

00:46:46.710 --> 00:46:48.180
<v Alex Blumberg>No, it doesn't.
<v Alex Blumberg>不，不会。

00:46:48.180 --> 00:46:49.390
<v Ira Glass>Why not?
<v Ira Glass>为什么不呢？

00:46:49.390 --> 00:47:05.810
<v David Kestenbaum>The federal deficit, that's when the federal government spends more money than it collects in taxes. So it has to borrow to cover the difference. That's with the deficit is, that borrowing. And the Fed is, as you said in your intro, a totally separate institution. It's not part of the federal government. So it hasn't nothing to do with this.
<v David Kestenbaum>联邦赤字，那是当联邦政府的花销超过其税收收入的时候。所以它必须通过借债来弥补差额。赤字就是这个，也就是借债。而美联储，正如你在导语中所说，是一个完全独立的机构。它不是联邦政府的一部分。所以它和这个毫无关系。

00:47:05.810 --> 00:47:14.950
<v Ira Glass>OK, but when the Fed loses money on some mall that it now owns, does that eventually in some sneaky back door way, come out of my taxes?
<v Ira Glass>好吧，但是当美联储在它现在拥有的某个购物中心上亏损时，最终会不会以某种偷偷摸摸的后门方式，从我的税收里扣除？

00:47:14.950 --> 00:47:38.950
<v David Kestenbaum>No. Because first of all, it's not part of the government. Second of all, even if it were, every year the Fed makes a profit. The Fed never loses money. It can create money out of thin air from nowhere. It uses that to buy government bonds, things that pay interest. So it gets interest every year. And every year it makes a huge profit, like in 2009 at the height of the crisis, it made $47 billion.
<v David Kestenbaum>不会。因为首先，它不是政府的一部分。其次，即使它是，美联储每年都在盈利。美联储从不亏钱。它可以凭空从虚无中创造出货币。它用这些钱来购买政府债券、那些支付利息的东西。所以它每年都能拿到利息。而且它每年都获得巨额利润，比如在危机最严重的2009年，它赚了470亿美元。

00:47:38.950 --> 00:47:44.310
<v Alex Blumberg>And that profit they always turn over to the federal government. So it actually reduces the deficit.
<v Alex Blumberg>而且他们总是把这些利润上交给联邦政府。所以它实际上减少了赤字。

00:47:44.310 --> 00:47:56.310
<v Ira Glass>And so all of this money that they've created since 2008, totaling it up if I understand right, it's over $1.6 trillion. Does this have anything to do with the TARP program to buy up toxic assets?
<v Ira Glass>那么，自从2008年以来他们创造的所有这些钱，如果我没理解错的话，总计超过1.6万亿美元。这与购买有毒资产的TARP计划有什么关系吗？

00:47:56.310 --> 00:47:58.110
<v David Kestenbaum>No, that was the federal government.
<v David Kestenbaum>没有，那是联邦政府的事。

00:47:58.110 --> 00:48:00.830
<v Ira Glass>Does it have anything to do with Obama's stimulus package.
<v Ira Glass>这和奥巴马的经济刺激计划有什么关系吗？

00:48:00.830 --> 00:48:03.010
<v David Kestenbaum>No, federal government.
<v David Kestenbaum>没有，也是联邦政府的。

00:48:03.010 --> 00:48:28.640
<v Ira Glass>But this $1.6 trillion is bigger than both of those combined. We have these huge fights that drag on for weeks over the stimulus package and over these other things, and then the Fed does something that dwarfs these in size. It's twice as big as either of those programs. And there's no discussion. The Congress doesn't debate it. The president doesn't approve it. There's no public input. Some eggheads in a room just kind of wave a wand and then it just happens?
<v Ira Glass>但这1.6万亿美元比这两者加起来还要大。我们为经济刺激计划和其他事情进行了旷日持久的巨大争论，持续了好几周，然后美联储做了一件事，在规模上把这些都比下去了。它比这两个计划中任何一个都要大两倍。而且没有任何讨论。国会不进行辩论。总统不予批准。没有任何公众意见。房间里的几个书呆子挥了挥魔杖，然后事情就发生了？

00:48:28.640 --> 00:48:35.110
<v David Kestenbaum>Yep. That's the idea of a central bank.
<v David Kestenbaum>对。这就是中央银行的概念。

00:48:35.110 --> 00:48:39.080
<v Ira Glass>OK, is that a good thing?
<v Ira Glass>好吧，这是件好事吗？

00:48:39.080 --> 00:48:57.740
<v Alex Blumberg>It depends who you talk to. Most economists would say, thank God the Fed did this. Thank God the Fed used its super power to create money out of thin air and lent that money to Harley Davidson and Goldman Sachs, and you and me to buy houses, and Bear Stearns in exchange for that mall in Oklahoma. Doing all that crazy crap, that kept us out of soup lines.
<v Alex Blumberg>这取决于你跟谁谈。大多数经济学家会说，谢天谢地美联储做了这件事。谢天谢地美联储动用了它的超能力，凭空制造出货币，把钱借给了哈雷戴维森和高盛，借给你和我去买房子，还借给贝尔斯登以换取俄克拉荷马州的那个购物中心。搞了那么多疯狂的烂摊子，这让我们免于陷入排队领救济粮的境地。

00:48:57.740 --> 00:49:23.330
<v David Kestenbaum>And the Chairman of the Federal Reserve, Ben Bernanke, says that because of the Fed's actions, quote, "The world was spared a cataclysm that could have rivaled or surpassed the Great Depression." And Bernanke is actually one of the leading scholars on the economics of the Great Depression. And he says, in fact, that the Great Depression happened in large part because the Fed back then wasn't crazy enough. Or as he put it, they were quote, "Insufficiently willing to challenge the orthodoxies of their day."
<v David Kestenbaum>美联储主席本·伯南克（Ben Bernanke）表示，由于美联储的行动，引述：“世界免于遭受一场可与大萧条匹敌甚至超越大萧条的灾难。”伯南克实际上是大萧条经济学领域的顶尖学者之一。他甚至表示，大萧条之所以发生，很大程度上是因为当年的美联储不够疯狂。或者用他的话说，他们引述：“不够乐意去挑战他们那个时代的教条。”

00:49:23.330 --> 00:49:34.130
<v David Kestenbaum>Alan Blinder is in this camp. He's an economist at Princeton and the former Vice Chairman of the Fed. And he says he's glad Bernanke was willing to challenge the orthodoxy of our day. To take the Fed to Crazytown.
<v David Kestenbaum>阿兰·布林德（Alan Blinder）就属于这一阵营。他是普林斯顿大学的经济学家，也是美联储前副主席。他说他很高兴伯南克愿意挑战我们这个时代的教条。把美联储带到疯狂小镇。

00:49:34.130 --> 00:50:07.840
<v Alan Blinder>But that is a very good thing that that happened. These very, very unusual things that the Fed did are things that are either never going to be repeated again in your lifetime, or if they are, it'll probably be a long time from now. Once we get back to normal, this is not going to be part of the Fed's everyday way of doing business. It will go back to the boring thing of creating or destroying money by buying or selling treasury bills.
<v Alan Blinder>但这绝对是件大好事。美联储所做的这些非常非常不同寻常的事情，要么在你的一生中永远不会再次发生，要么如果再次发生，那可能也是很久以后的事了。一旦我们恢复正常，这将不再是美联储日常业务的一部分。它将回归到通过买卖国库券来创造或消灭货币的无聊事情中去。

00:50:07.840 --> 00:50:23.860
<v David Kestenbaum>There's a bunch of people who disagree with Alan Blinder on this though, and we're going to be hearing a lot from them in the coming months. Maybe you've followed some of the news about how three quarters of the House of Representatives-- democrats and republicans alike-- voted for more scrutiny of the Fed. That happened because of this guy.
<v David Kestenbaum>不过，有一群人对此并不同意阿兰·布林德的看法，在接下来的几个月里，我们将经常听到他们的声音。也许你关注过一些新闻，关于众议院四分之三的议员——民主党人和共和党人一样——如何投票支持对美联储进行更多审查。这都是因为这个人。

00:50:23.860 --> 00:50:26.490
<v Chairman>Gentleman, Texas Mr. Paul for three minutes.
<v Chairman>先生们，德克萨斯州的保罗先生三分钟。

00:50:26.490 --> 00:50:30.190
<v Ron Paul>Thank you, Mr. Chairman. I want to thank you for calling this hearing.
<v Ron Paul>谢谢你，主席先生。我要感谢你召集这次听证会。

00:50:30.190 --> 00:50:37.790
<v Alex Blumberg>Ron Paul, the libertarian-leaning republican congressmen from Texas is the go-to guy in Congress if you're looking for someone to criticize the Federal Reserve.
<v Alex Blumberg>罗恩·保罗（Ron Paul），这位来自德克萨斯州的具有自由意志主义倾向的共和党国会议员，如果你想找人批评美联储，他就是国会中的首选人物。

00:50:37.790 --> 00:51:03.930
<v Ron Paul>The question we in the Congress have to ask is, why is it that the Congress is so eager to give up their prerogatives and their responsibilities? Whether it's giving the executive branch the authority to go to war without the Congress saying much, or whether it's turning over the monetary system to somebody that can operate essentially in secrecy and deal not with a few hundred billion dollars like $800 billion here and there, but trillions of dollars when it adds up.
<v Ron Paul>我们国会必须提出的问题是，为什么国会如此急于放弃他们的特权和责任？无论是赋予行政部门在国会没怎么表态的情况下就去打仗的权力，还是把货币系统交托给某个可以基本上在保密状态下运作的人，去处理的不是几千亿美元——比如这里和那里的8000亿美元——而是累积起来达到数万亿美元的资金。

00:51:03.930 --> 00:51:18.550
<v Alex Blumberg>For decades, Paul has been on the campaign to abolish the Federal Reserve. And it was lonely campaign. But with the republicans taking over the House of Representatives, Ron Paul, author of a book called End the Fed is now the head of the committee, which oversees the Fed.
<v Alex Blumberg>几十年来，保罗一直致力于废除美联储的运动。这是一场孤独的运动。但随着共和党接管众议院，著有《终结美联储》一书的罗恩·保罗现在成为了监督美联储的委员会的主席。

00:51:18.550 --> 00:51:37.570
<v David Kestenbaum>And the main beef that he and lots of people have with the Fed is that it's unclear the Fed really knows what it's doing. US economy they say is too big and too complex to be managed by a group of PhDs who meet every six weeks, no matter how smart they are. By trying to manage something that can't be managed, the Fed does more damage to the economy than good.
<v David Kestenbaum>他和许多人对美联储的主要不满在于，美联储是否真的知道自己在做什么并不明朗。他们说，美国经济太大、太复杂，不是一群每六周开一次会的博士所能管理的，不管他们有多聪明。通过试图管理无法管理的东西，美联储对经济造成的损害大于益处。

00:51:37.570 --> 00:51:46.010
<v David Kestenbaum>One of the leading critics of the Fed, an economist named George Selgin says the Fed is like a fireman who gets credit for putting out fires that he helped start.
<v David Kestenbaum>美联储的主要批评者之一、经济学家乔治·塞尔金（George Selgin）表示，美联储就像一个消防员，他扑灭了自己参与纵火的火灾，却因此获得了赞誉。

00:51:46.010 --> 00:52:09.900
<v Alex Blumberg>And Selgin has a point. Take our current recession. Even if you grant that the Fed helped get us out of it, most economists and not just Fed critics say that it was Fed actions earlier in the decade, holding interest rates too low for too long, which helped create the housing bubble, which got us into this mess in the first place.
<v Alex Blumberg>塞尔金的话有道理。以我们当前的经济衰退为例。即使你承认美联储帮助我们摆脱了困境，大多数经济学家（不仅仅是美联储的批评者）也表示，正是美联储在十年前采取的行动——将利率维持得太低、时间持续得太长——助长了房地产泡沫，而正是这个泡沫最初让我们陷入了这种困境。

00:52:09.900 --> 00:52:24.230
<v David Kestenbaum>So today, the Fed is in uncharted territory. Instead of making little movements to the super tanker's steering wheel, the Fed has spun it, leaving us all with two questions. What direction us the tanker going to go as a result? And do we trust the person at the helm?
<v David Kestenbaum>所以今天，美联储正处于未知的领域。美联储没有对超级油轮的方向盘进行微调，而是猛打方向盘，给我们所有人都留下两个问题。作为结果，这艘油轮将驶向哪个方向？我们信任掌舵的人吗？

00:52:24.230 --> 00:52:37.400
<v Alex Blumberg>The Fed has created a huge, unprecedented amount of money, thrown it into the economy. Now they have to figure out how to get it out before it causes inflation. The Fed has gone a little crazy. Once you go crazy, how do you get back to being boring?
<v Alex Blumberg>美联储创造了巨大、史无前例的资金量，并将其投入到经济中。现在他们必须弄清楚如何在它引起通货膨胀之前把它收回来。美联储变得有点疯狂了。一旦你疯狂了，你怎么才能恢复到无聊的状态？

00:52:37.400 --> 00:52:45.020
<v David Kestenbaum>And trustworthy? Remember the value of our money depends on that trust. Again, Vincent Reinhart talking to our colleague Chana Joffe-Walt.
<v David Kestenbaum>而且值得信赖？记住，我们货币的价值取决于这种信任。再次听听文森特·莱inhart与我们的同事查娜·乔夫-沃尔特的对话。

00:52:45.020 --> 00:52:56.610
<v Vincent Reinhart>Because you trust the Fed and because you know everybody else trusts the Fed, it is valued in markets because other people value it.
<v Vincent Reinhart>因为你信任美联储，并且因为你知道其他人也都信任美联储，所以它在市场上才有价值，因为其他人看重它。

00:52:56.610 --> 00:53:07.880
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So really, that's the biggest thing that the Fed has going for it. It's not the gold in the basement, it's not the marble floors. The most important thing to them is just that everyone trusts them.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以实际上，这是美联储拥有的最大资本。不是地下室里的黄金，也不是大理石地板。对他们来说最重要的是，每个人都信任他们。

00:53:07.880 --> 00:53:12.340
<v Vincent Reinhart>The magic of central banking is it works on trust.
<v Vincent Reinhart>中央银行的魔力在于它是建立在信任之上的。

00:53:12.340 --> 00:53:24.270
<v Alex Blumberg>Once that trust is gone, the value of money goes away too. It becomes just another stone on the bottom of the ocean.
<v Alex Blumberg>一旦这种信任消失，货币的价值也会随之消失。它就会变成海底的又一块普通石头。

00:53:24.270 --> 00:53:57.270
<v David Kestenbaum>The Fed knows this of course. That the weirder they act, the more they throw the whole enterprise into question. And this explains one of the biggest mysteries of all about visiting the Fed, why everyone is so, so boring. Their public pronouncements are famous for bureaucratic inscrutability. And I mean, here they are creating trillions of dollars out of thin air, doing things that in the history of central banking have never been done before. And in all our dealings with them, they are-- and you'll have to trust us on this, we'll spare you the tape-- studiously going out of their way to be as unexciting as possible.
<v David Kestenbaum>美联储当然知道这一点。他们的行为越古怪，就越会让整个机构受到性质上的质疑。这就解释了参观美联储时最大的谜团之一：为什么每个人都如此、如此无聊。他们的公开声明以官僚主义的晦涩难懂而闻名。我的意思是，他们在这里凭空创造了数万亿美元，做着中央银行历史上从未做过的事情。而在我们与他们的所有交往中，他们——你必须相信我们，我们就省下录音不放了——刻意绞尽脑汁地表现得尽可能平淡无奇。

00:53:57.270 --> 00:54:08.940
<v Alex Blumberg>Case in point, remember the mortgaged back security program? One and a quarter trillion dollars. That number was essentially pulled out of thin air. And yet, once the Fed officials in charge of purchasing those securities were given the number--
<v Alex Blumberg>一个典型的例子，还记得那个抵押贷款支持证券计划吗？一万二千五百亿美元。这个数字基本上是凭空捏造出来的。然而，一旦负责购买这些证券的美联储官员被赋予了这个数字——

00:54:08.940 --> 00:54:12.570
<v Dave Kestenbaum>They hit it exactly. Well, almost.
<v Dave Kestenbaum>他们分毫不差地完成了。嗯，几乎是。

00:54:12.570 --> 00:54:14.380
<v Male Speaker 3>I think we fell short by like--
<v Male Speaker 3>我想我们只差了大概——

00:54:14.380 --> 00:54:18.340
<v Vincent Reinhart>$0.61 short. $0.61.
<v Vincent Reinhart>差了0.61美元。0.61美元。

00:54:18.340 --> 00:54:19.180
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>什么？

00:54:19.180 --> 00:54:20.110
<v Vincent Reinhart>Seriously.
<v Vincent Reinhart>真的。

00:54:20.110 --> 00:54:23.710
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>No way. $1.25 trillion, you got within how much?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>开玩笑吧。1.25万亿美元，你们居然精确到这种程度？

00:54:23.710 --> 00:54:49.610
<v Vincent Reinhart>We got very, very close to the actual $1.25 trillion. I do think we were within pennies of the final charge. We didn't want to go over $1.25 trillion because $1.25 trillion was our mandate. We don't want to ever go beyond our mandate. And so if we erred, we wanted to err on the shy side.
<v Vincent Reinhart>我们非常非常接近实际的1.25万亿美元。我确实认为我们距离最终的花费只差几美分。我们不想超过1.25万亿美元，因为1.25万亿美元是我们的授权。我们永远不想超出我们的授权。所以如果我们有误差，我们宁愿偏向保守的一侧。

00:54:49.610 --> 00:55:07.040
<v David Kestenbaum>Whether you like it or not, this is our system. Empower a secret society of PhD with the ability to create trillions of dollars out of nothing and hope we can trust them to get it right and stay as boring as humanly possible.
<v David Kestenbaum>不管你喜欢与否，这就是我们的系统。赋予一个由博士组成的秘密社团凭空创造数万亿美元的能力，并希望我们可以相信他们能把事情做好，并保持人类所能达到的极致无聊。

00:55:07.040 --> 00:55:23.510
<v Ira Glass>Alex Blumberg and David Kestenbaum. They're both part of our Planet Money team, along with Chana Joffe-Walt and all the other reporters you've heard this hour. Their free podcast and their blog explain the stuff that all of us have never understood more entertainingly than seems fair. You can find all that at NPR .org/money.
<v Ira Glass>亚历克斯·布隆伯格（Alex Blumberg）和大卫·克斯滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum）。他们都是我们“星球金钱”团队的成员，还有查娜·乔夫-沃尔特以及你在这一小时里听到的所有其他记者。他们的免费播客和博客以一种看似不太公平的有趣方式，解释了我们所有人从未真正理解的东西。你可以在 NPR .org/money 上找到所有这些内容。

